1
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:08,920
I decided to cover both open problems in deep reinforcement

2
00:00:08,920 --> 00:00:12,900
learning and how to do deep RL research

3
00:00:12,900 --> 00:00:16,280
So let's start with part 1 and talking about open problems

4
00:00:16,280 --> 00:00:19,640
and frontiers in deep reinforcement learning

5
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:22,040
I'm going to break this into a few different categories

6
00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:25,840
The first is challenges on defining the problem setup

7
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:28,100
The second is challenges in methods

8
00:00:28,100 --> 00:00:32,960
And the third is challenges in deployment and evaluation

9
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:35,740
I should also mention that this will not be exhaustive

10
00:00:35,740 --> 00:00:40,160
There's many problems and many open challenges

11
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:41,860
in deep reinforcement learning

12
00:00:41,860 --> 00:00:44,960
I'm just picking out some of the ones that I think I've either

13
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:47,960
gotten questions about quite a bit or some of the problems

14
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:50,240
that I think are the most exciting

15
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:54,160
and the most interesting

16
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:55,280
So let's dive in

17
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:58,720
And in particular, in terms of some

18
00:00:58,720 --> 00:01:02,130
of the challenges in how we set up the deep RL problem

19
00:01:02,130 --> 00:01:04,690
we've seen a lot of successes in domains

20
00:01:04,690 --> 00:01:07,410
like games, math, reasoning, coding problems, where you can

21
00:01:07,410 --> 00:01:09,430
very easily define the reward

22
00:01:09,430 --> 00:01:12,290
You can very easily verify whether or not

23
00:01:12,290 --> 00:01:15,610
the reinforcement learning system did the correct thing

24
00:01:15,610 --> 00:01:17,290
which is great

25
00:01:17,290 --> 00:01:21,690
But in many real-world scenarios, rewards don't exist

26
00:01:21,690 --> 00:01:25,090
Or rewards are very delayed

27
00:01:25,090 --> 00:01:30,450
And this introduces challenges in the typical problem setting

28
00:01:30,450 --> 00:01:33,410
And so, for example, let's think about language models

29
00:01:33,410 --> 00:01:35,850
for chatbots

30
00:01:35,850 --> 00:01:38,950
Right now, we have preference optimization

31
00:01:38,950 --> 00:01:41,370
And one might actually say that preference optimization

32
00:01:41,370 --> 00:01:43,970
for chatbots seems to work quite well

33
00:01:43,970 --> 00:01:47,120
We're able to actually deploy reinforcement learning at scale

34
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:50,810
But there's actually pretty big problems

35
00:01:50,810 --> 00:01:52,450
with preference optimization

36
00:01:52,450 --> 00:01:54,930
And as one example, there was this research paper

37
00:01:54,930 --> 00:02:00,590
by Sharma and Tong that was evaluating what

38
00:02:00,590 --> 00:02:02,270
preferences people are giving

39
00:02:02,270 --> 00:02:06,190
And the x-axis is the probability

40
00:02:06,190 --> 00:02:07,830
that a response with the given feature

41
00:02:07,830 --> 00:02:11,030
is preferred when all else is equal

42
00:02:11,030 --> 00:02:15,910
And there's different things here on the left axis

43
00:02:15,910 --> 00:02:19,310
And the things that were most preferred

44
00:02:19,310 --> 00:02:24,310
in the human preferences were whether it was something

45
00:02:24,310 --> 00:02:27,590
that the human agreed with and whether or not the response

46
00:02:27,590 --> 00:02:29,250
was confident

47
00:02:29,250 --> 00:02:31,790
So essentially, the things that people are giving preferences

48
00:02:31,790 --> 00:02:35,350
for are whether or not the language model response

49
00:02:35,350 --> 00:02:38,210
is something that's confidently saying something

50
00:02:38,210 --> 00:02:39,830
and whether that thing that it's saying

51
00:02:39,830 --> 00:02:42,510
agrees with what the person thinks

52
00:02:42,510 --> 00:02:45,610
And then all the way down here is factuality

53
00:02:45,610 --> 00:02:47,110
And so they're actually caring about

54
00:02:47,110 --> 00:02:49,750
whether it's confident more than whether or not the response is

55
00:02:49,750 --> 00:02:53,230
actually correct or true

56
00:02:53,230 --> 00:02:56,000
And so people aren't actually giving good preferences

57
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,840
Arguably, I think we would want language models

58
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:03,000
to be valuing factuality over things like confidence

59
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,520
And we're more optimizing for what people kind of want

60
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:12,360
to hear rather than the kinds of responses that will be good

61
00:03:12,360 --> 00:03:14,640
Now, if you also think about preference optimization

62
00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:17,120
for personalization, for example

63
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:20,690
we might want to develop language models that

64
00:03:20,690 --> 00:03:22,400
are telling people what they want to hear

65
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:23,780
and the kinds of recommendations

66
00:03:23,780 --> 00:03:27,600
for example, that are closely personalized to their interests

67
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:29,200
and their beliefs

68
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:31,160
But on the flip side, if we personalize

69
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:33,720
these kinds of models for the many people that

70
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:35,520
use these models, then it might lead

71
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:38,720
to polarization in people who, for example, have

72
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:41,440
extreme beliefs rather than being exposed

73
00:03:41,440 --> 00:03:43,100
to a wide range of beliefs

74
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:45,240
And so even something like personalization

75
00:03:45,240 --> 00:03:48,820
may not intrinsically be what we want to optimize for

76
00:03:48,820 --> 00:03:50,240
And at the end of the day, there's

77
00:03:50,240 --> 00:03:53,620
typically multiple things that we do care about optimizing for

78
00:03:53,620 --> 00:03:56,140
And balancing those different objectives that may actually

79
00:03:56,140 --> 00:03:58,420
compete with each other ends up being

80
00:03:58,420 --> 00:04:01,500
a very challenging problem

81
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:03,060
So this whole kind of realm of how

82
00:04:03,060 --> 00:04:06,300
do we define rewards and the kind of objective

83
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:10,100
as a whole for language models is--

84
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:11,840
in many ways, there's a lot of progress

85
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:16,500
But it's still a really challenging problem

86
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:20,410
Now, separate from language models, there's also robotics

87
00:04:20,410 --> 00:04:22,740
Right now, in robotics, either you

88
00:04:22,740 --> 00:04:25,260
have binary rewards in the real world

89
00:04:25,260 --> 00:04:28,300
or, if in simulation, you might have hand-shaped rewards

90
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:30,860
where you define different terms for what you want

91
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:34,400
This takes a lot of effort, ends up being fairly challenging

92
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:38,020
or takes a lot of labeling annotations

93
00:04:38,020 --> 00:04:40,820
And even then, even when you have human annotations

94
00:04:40,820 --> 00:04:44,080
it can be tricky to judge rewards

95
00:04:44,080 --> 00:04:47,340
And so, for example, if you were to look at these shirts that

96
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:52,310
were each folded by a robot, if you were to think about which

97
00:04:52,310 --> 00:04:57,470
shirt was folded the best or try to give rewards for these shirt

98
00:04:57,470 --> 00:04:59,430
folds, it's rather tricky to figure out

99
00:04:59,430 --> 00:05:02,390
how you might reward different mistakes, different amounts

100
00:05:02,390 --> 00:05:07,110
of crinkleness, whether or not the shirt is off center or not

101
00:05:07,110 --> 00:05:10,230
It's generally a very ambiguous problem and hard even for people

102
00:05:10,230 --> 00:05:16,430
to think about how to provide rewards for these systems

103
00:05:16,430 --> 00:05:20,470
Another example-- let's think about YouTube recommendations

104
00:05:20,470 --> 00:05:22,550
What I mean here is if when you're on YouTube

105
00:05:22,550 --> 00:05:24,630
there's recommendations for a next video

106
00:05:24,630 --> 00:05:27,510
to watch after watching your current video

107
00:05:27,510 --> 00:05:34,510
And these an algorithm that selects

108
00:05:34,510 --> 00:05:37,510
what these recommendations should be

109
00:05:37,510 --> 00:05:40,610
Now, we don't have exact details on how this algorithm works

110
00:05:40,610 --> 00:05:43,670
But there was a research paper that

111
00:05:43,670 --> 00:05:46,710
actually was talking a little bit about how

112
00:05:46,710 --> 00:05:48,870
they do these recommendations

113
00:05:48,870 --> 00:05:52,030
And as you might expect, when they do these recommendations

114
00:05:52,030 --> 00:05:53,570
they want to optimize for engagement

115
00:05:53,570 --> 00:05:55,280
which is like whether or not you actually

116
00:05:55,280 --> 00:05:56,690
clicked on the recommendation

117
00:05:56,690 --> 00:05:58,400
And if you clicked on the recommendation

118
00:05:58,400 --> 00:06:00,320
that was probably a good recommendation

119
00:06:00,320 --> 00:06:02,990
And also, satisfaction, which is that after you clicked on that

120
00:06:02,990 --> 00:06:05,250
if you actually like the video, then that's

121
00:06:05,250 --> 00:06:08,050
also a good sign that it was a good recommendation

122
00:06:08,050 --> 00:06:10,850
So this all seems reasonable

123
00:06:10,850 --> 00:06:14,050
But another thing that was mentioned in the research paper

124
00:06:14,050 --> 00:06:18,290
was that the combination of these metrics for the reward

125
00:06:18,290 --> 00:06:20,650
function was actually manually tuned

126
00:06:20,650 --> 00:06:23,770
by a person rather than something

127
00:06:23,770 --> 00:06:26,290
that is designed automatically

128
00:06:26,290 --> 00:06:29,890
This kind of also goes to show the challenge

129
00:06:29,890 --> 00:06:32,370
in actually defining these kinds of reward functions

130
00:06:32,370 --> 00:06:34,690
when we don't know how best to balance

131
00:06:34,690 --> 00:06:40,650
these kinds of objectives

132
00:06:40,650 --> 00:06:42,230
We talked a bit about math reasoning

133
00:06:42,230 --> 00:06:43,650
If we want to take those advances

134
00:06:43,650 --> 00:06:47,450
and apply them to broader scientific experimentation

135
00:06:47,450 --> 00:06:49,340
and reasoning, you often don't necessarily

136
00:06:49,340 --> 00:06:52,500
know what the final answer should be

137
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:55,240
And so even training on math reasoning

138
00:06:55,240 --> 00:06:57,560
you might see some transfer to scientific reasoning

139
00:06:57,560 --> 00:06:59,100
But if you want to train on a broader

140
00:06:59,100 --> 00:07:01,460
set of scientific reasoning problems

141
00:07:01,460 --> 00:07:05,140
this sort of verification becomes challenging

142
00:07:05,140 --> 00:07:06,980
And the last thing I'll mention along

143
00:07:06,980 --> 00:07:09,700
these sorts of rewards and verification

144
00:07:09,700 --> 00:07:13,020
is that if we think about how humans learn

145
00:07:13,020 --> 00:07:16,220
humans often learn by rolling around on the floor

146
00:07:16,220 --> 00:07:18,180
playing with random toys

147
00:07:18,180 --> 00:07:22,100
And it seems like they're not just optimizing

148
00:07:22,100 --> 00:07:23,820
for one particular task

149
00:07:23,820 --> 00:07:25,460
They're optimizing-- well, who knows

150
00:07:25,460 --> 00:07:28,830
what they're optimizing for

151
00:07:28,830 --> 00:07:30,500
For example, we could develop algorithms

152
00:07:30,500 --> 00:07:32,980
that would allow machines to explore in this way

153
00:07:32,980 --> 00:07:34,980
potentially optimizing for learning, potentially

154
00:07:34,980 --> 00:07:36,940
optimizing for exploration

155
00:07:36,940 --> 00:07:41,500
And this is certainly an open problem

156
00:07:41,500 --> 00:07:44,700
So that's on the problem setup

157
00:07:44,700 --> 00:07:46,880
Now, let's talk a little bit about challenges

158
00:07:46,880 --> 00:07:49,160
on the method side

159
00:07:49,160 --> 00:07:53,240
So the first topic I wanted to bring up

160
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:56,040
was leveraging prior data or knowledge

161
00:07:56,040 --> 00:07:58,000
So there's a lot of circumstances

162
00:07:58,000 --> 00:07:59,700
where we're not starting reinforcement

163
00:07:59,700 --> 00:08:00,620
learning from scratch

164
00:08:00,620 --> 00:08:03,480
And we have some prior data, some prior knowledge

165
00:08:03,480 --> 00:08:05,600
about what is best to explore

166
00:08:05,600 --> 00:08:08,520
And the default tool for leveraging previous knowledge

167
00:08:08,520 --> 00:08:11,100
and data is to use pre-trained model weights

168
00:08:11,100 --> 00:08:14,160
or to initialize your replay buffer with any previous data

169
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:16,800
that you have

170
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:20,280
But this tool doesn't extend to all sorts of scenarios

171
00:08:20,280 --> 00:08:21,660
we might care about

172
00:08:21,660 --> 00:08:23,240
There might be scenarios where we have

173
00:08:23,240 --> 00:08:25,180
more abstract prior knowledge

174
00:08:25,180 --> 00:08:26,680
Maybe we have some knowledge that we

175
00:08:26,680 --> 00:08:28,720
read from news articles that isn't kind

176
00:08:28,720 --> 00:08:30,500
of in the typical data format

177
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:33,820
We want to incorporate those facts into our model

178
00:08:33,820 --> 00:08:36,320
Maybe we have some hints that could be

179
00:08:36,320 --> 00:08:40,120
helpful for solving a problem

180
00:08:40,120 --> 00:08:42,320
And even separate from that, if we

181
00:08:42,320 --> 00:08:45,010
think about this kind of paradigm

182
00:08:45,010 --> 00:08:48,250
of using pre-training and initialized data sets

183
00:08:48,250 --> 00:08:50,130
there are scenarios where this might actually

184
00:08:50,130 --> 00:08:52,410
be too constraining

185
00:08:52,410 --> 00:08:56,590
And so as one example, here's a research paper by Ball et al

186
00:08:56,590 --> 00:08:58,330
In 2023

187
00:08:58,330 --> 00:08:59,770
And they introduced a method that

188
00:08:59,770 --> 00:09:02,410
was studying using prior demonstration data

189
00:09:02,410 --> 00:09:04,370
with reinforcement learning

190
00:09:04,370 --> 00:09:07,170
And the method that they introduced

191
00:09:07,170 --> 00:09:09,390
they initialized the buffer

192
00:09:09,390 --> 00:09:11,650
But they actually didn't pre-train the weights

193
00:09:11,650 --> 00:09:13,480
And they found that pre-training the weights

194
00:09:13,480 --> 00:09:17,050
did worse than not pre-training the weights

195
00:09:17,050 --> 00:09:19,450
And so this kind of suggests that there

196
00:09:19,450 --> 00:09:22,770
might be scenarios where using pre-trained weights

197
00:09:22,770 --> 00:09:24,970
or using pre-trained data sets might actually be

198
00:09:24,970 --> 00:09:27,970
too constraining on our model

199
00:09:27,970 --> 00:09:32,690
As one example of this, if you have a pre-trained model

200
00:09:32,690 --> 00:09:34,850
the output layer of that pre-trained model

201
00:09:34,850 --> 00:09:37,210
might have converged to something fairly deterministic

202
00:09:37,210 --> 00:09:39,210
And then if you're trying to learn something new

203
00:09:39,210 --> 00:09:44,030
or adapt it in a new way, then that last layer

204
00:09:44,030 --> 00:09:47,470
might not actually be exploring in a good way

205
00:09:47,470 --> 00:09:51,590
So that's one example of why you might see worse performance

206
00:09:51,590 --> 00:09:56,070
if you initialize your weights with some previous neural

207
00:09:56,070 --> 00:09:57,590
network

208
00:09:57,590 --> 00:09:59,670
And arguably, in language models

209
00:09:59,670 --> 00:10:02,310
perhaps reinforcement learning works quite well

210
00:10:02,310 --> 00:10:05,670
because pre-trained models often have a very diverse distribution

211
00:10:05,670 --> 00:10:08,630
over output tokens

212
00:10:08,630 --> 00:10:12,270
And in terms of leveraging prior data and knowledge

213
00:10:12,270 --> 00:10:14,050
and also being able to go beyond it

214
00:10:14,050 --> 00:10:16,510
I think that this question is really important for thinking

215
00:10:16,510 --> 00:10:19,070
about how language models can solve problems that humans

216
00:10:19,070 --> 00:10:21,310
haven't solved because they probably

217
00:10:21,310 --> 00:10:24,050
should build upon the knowledge in the pre-trained data sets

218
00:10:24,050 --> 00:10:26,390
But they also need to think about how to go beyond that

219
00:10:26,390 --> 00:10:29,510
and not be too constrained by what the pre-trained model might

220
00:10:29,510 --> 00:10:31,270
say

221
00:10:31,270 --> 00:10:34,070
And it's also quite useful for thinking

222
00:10:34,070 --> 00:10:36,950
about how robots or autonomous vehicles might

223
00:10:36,950 --> 00:10:40,190
learn how to do things faster and more reliably

224
00:10:40,190 --> 00:10:41,660
than what humans do

225
00:10:41,660 --> 00:10:44,360
And perhaps if we constrain too much

226
00:10:44,360 --> 00:10:47,760
to the pre-trained knowledge or the prior knowledge

227
00:10:47,760 --> 00:10:50,400
they might not be able to surpass human abilities in terms

228
00:10:50,400 --> 00:10:53,370
of speed or reliability

229
00:10:53,370 --> 00:10:55,120
So I think, generally, these two questions

230
00:10:55,120 --> 00:10:56,840
are really fascinating

231
00:10:56,840 --> 00:11:00,880
And we've seen some suggestions that reinforcement learning

232
00:11:00,880 --> 00:11:01,760
can do this

233
00:11:01,760 --> 00:11:04,320
And it's certainly one of the most promising tools for going

234
00:11:04,320 --> 00:11:07,040
beyond what humans can do

235
00:11:07,040 --> 00:11:12,080
But lots of interesting and open work to do there

236
00:11:12,080 --> 00:11:14,360
Now, one question I've gotten quite a bit

237
00:11:14,360 --> 00:11:17,640
is whether we can leverage world models and video generation

238
00:11:17,640 --> 00:11:21,560
models for robotics and control

239
00:11:21,560 --> 00:11:24,840
And we've seen some really impressive results, especially

240
00:11:24,840 --> 00:11:27,340
recently from video generation models

241
00:11:27,340 --> 00:11:30,070
where they clearly have a lot of rich knowledge about the world

242
00:11:30,070 --> 00:11:34,680
like how cooking on a stove works, how typing on a keyboard

243
00:11:34,680 --> 00:11:38,640
works, and how that might look like if the keyboard was made

244
00:11:38,640 --> 00:11:43,260
of gummy bears, and also how things like even walking

245
00:11:43,260 --> 00:11:45,600
at an abstract level work

246
00:11:45,600 --> 00:11:47,220
So it seems like this sort of knowledge

247
00:11:47,220 --> 00:11:51,780
should be really useful for learning behavior

248
00:11:51,780 --> 00:11:53,620
But there's actually a lot of nuance

249
00:11:53,620 --> 00:11:57,900
and a lot of challenges with using these kinds of models

250
00:11:57,900 --> 00:12:02,780
As an example, if you train an action-conditioned video

251
00:12:02,780 --> 00:12:04,240
generation model--

252
00:12:04,240 --> 00:12:07,140
so a model that takes as input the current image

253
00:12:07,140 --> 00:12:11,100
and future actions and predicts future video--

254
00:12:11,100 --> 00:12:14,420
and if you train that on demonstrations and rollouts

255
00:12:14,420 --> 00:12:19,700
from one policy and then you evaluate a new policy's

256
00:12:19,700 --> 00:12:23,980
actions with that model and evaluate whether or not that

257
00:12:23,980 --> 00:12:27,180
those actions lead to good outcomes

258
00:12:27,180 --> 00:12:29,940
then these actions won't necessarily

259
00:12:29,940 --> 00:12:34,440
be represented in the data that the model was trained on

260
00:12:34,440 --> 00:12:38,070
And as a result, the model often will make inaccurate predictions

261
00:12:38,070 --> 00:12:40,230
for these actions

262
00:12:40,230 --> 00:12:42,210
If most of the data set is demonstrations

263
00:12:42,210 --> 00:12:43,670
then it will predict a video that

264
00:12:43,670 --> 00:12:45,730
corresponds to correctly doing the task

265
00:12:45,730 --> 00:12:48,950
even if these actions did not lead to a correct outcome

266
00:12:48,950 --> 00:12:51,750
And generally, these models have very little data--

267
00:12:51,750 --> 00:12:54,750
for example, like narrowly making a mistake

268
00:12:54,750 --> 00:12:57,270
or making mistakes that are very different from the kinds

269
00:12:57,270 --> 00:13:00,710
of mistakes that humans make

270
00:13:00,710 --> 00:13:03,550
The other thing here is that even small physical inaccuracies

271
00:13:03,550 --> 00:13:05,990
can lead to poor performance

272
00:13:05,990 --> 00:13:09,550
And so a lot of times, these videos look really, really good

273
00:13:09,550 --> 00:13:11,130
They look like they might be accurate

274
00:13:11,130 --> 00:13:14,630
But that doesn't mean that the underlying physics is actually

275
00:13:14,630 --> 00:13:16,270
accurate

276
00:13:16,270 --> 00:13:18,270
Now, I think that there are solutions here

277
00:13:18,270 --> 00:13:20,350
One possible solution is to try to train

278
00:13:20,350 --> 00:13:22,470
on data from lots of different policies

279
00:13:22,470 --> 00:13:24,550
with this kind of approach, with the hopes

280
00:13:24,550 --> 00:13:28,190
that that will lead to enough coverage over action

281
00:13:28,190 --> 00:13:32,150
distributions to leverage these models

282
00:13:32,150 --> 00:13:33,750
And then the second possible solution

283
00:13:33,750 --> 00:13:36,450
is that we might be able to use these video generation

284
00:13:36,450 --> 00:13:39,170
models in other ways

285
00:13:39,170 --> 00:13:42,010
And one example of that, maybe instead

286
00:13:42,010 --> 00:13:46,090
of training this sort of physical model of the future

287
00:13:46,090 --> 00:13:51,090
we could generate, for example, what

288
00:13:51,090 --> 00:13:55,450
should the future video look like not conditioned on actions

289
00:13:55,450 --> 00:13:58,370
And if we train this only on demonstrations

290
00:13:58,370 --> 00:14:02,330
then, for example, we could predict future video and plan

291
00:14:02,330 --> 00:14:06,930
out how to accomplish the task in the future in video space

292
00:14:06,930 --> 00:14:10,090
and then run a goal conditioned policy

293
00:14:10,090 --> 00:14:14,170
using the predicted video

294
00:14:14,170 --> 00:14:16,690
And something like this kind of sidesteps the challenge

295
00:14:16,690 --> 00:14:18,610
of this sort of out-of-distribution action

296
00:14:18,610 --> 00:14:20,130
problem because you're only using

297
00:14:20,130 --> 00:14:23,770
the model for a form of planning where you're thinking

298
00:14:23,770 --> 00:14:26,170
through what is a good outcome in the future

299
00:14:26,170 --> 00:14:27,690
rather than thinking through what

300
00:14:27,690 --> 00:14:34,440
is the outcome of specific actions

301
00:14:34,440 --> 00:14:36,630
And then the third thing on the method side--

302
00:14:36,630 --> 00:14:38,300
the third challenge that I'll talk about

303
00:14:38,300 --> 00:14:41,020
is thinking about scaling

304
00:14:41,020 --> 00:14:46,900
So on the scaling front, we've seen large-scale RL

305
00:14:46,900 --> 00:14:49,040
succeed with language models

306
00:14:49,040 --> 00:14:52,420
And that's actually really exciting to see

307
00:14:52,420 --> 00:14:54,780
But there's still shortcomings there

308
00:14:54,780 --> 00:14:58,660
So a lot of language model RL is considering

309
00:14:58,660 --> 00:15:02,860
problems that are still fairly short horizon or extremely

310
00:15:02,860 --> 00:15:06,180
online, like generating rollouts of the model

311
00:15:06,180 --> 00:15:09,620
every single iteration

312
00:15:09,620 --> 00:15:12,060
And as an example of this, if we're

313
00:15:12,060 --> 00:15:14,540
doing preference optimization, typically we

314
00:15:14,540 --> 00:15:16,860
consider this single turn dialogue

315
00:15:16,860 --> 00:15:19,060
setting, where we're getting rewards just

316
00:15:19,060 --> 00:15:21,700
for our next response rather than

317
00:15:21,700 --> 00:15:25,980
trying to reward the outcome of the full conversation

318
00:15:25,980 --> 00:15:28,690
And this means that it's a shorter horizon problem

319
00:15:28,690 --> 00:15:31,150
It means you don't have to have a human in the loop of data

320
00:15:31,150 --> 00:15:34,990
collection to generate those dialogue conversations

321
00:15:34,990 --> 00:15:37,870
And so this makes the problem easier

322
00:15:37,870 --> 00:15:39,770
which is good to make the problem easier

323
00:15:39,770 --> 00:15:42,350
But it means that if there are scenarios where you have longer

324
00:15:42,350 --> 00:15:44,990
horizon problems or you need a human in the loop of data

325
00:15:44,990 --> 00:15:48,150
collection, that becomes a lot more challenging to do

326
00:15:48,150 --> 00:15:51,270
at large scale

327
00:15:51,270 --> 00:15:53,550
As another example, if we think about reasoning

328
00:15:53,550 --> 00:15:56,830
for math problems, this relies on a very large number

329
00:15:56,830 --> 00:16:02,870
of online samples from the model interleaved with policy updates

330
00:16:02,870 --> 00:16:05,510
And there are some scenarios where it's actually really hard

331
00:16:05,510 --> 00:16:09,650
to collect that very online data interleaved with policy updates

332
00:16:09,650 --> 00:16:12,830
especially if collecting data involves interfacing with humans

333
00:16:12,830 --> 00:16:17,870
or interfacing with the physical world

334
00:16:17,870 --> 00:16:21,390
So towards this end, if we wanted

335
00:16:21,390 --> 00:16:24,230
to think about longer horizon problems

336
00:16:24,230 --> 00:16:26,650
and efficiently learning, tools like value functions

337
00:16:26,650 --> 00:16:31,570
should be really useful

338
00:16:31,570 --> 00:16:34,010
We've seen some signs of life in training value functions

339
00:16:34,010 --> 00:16:34,750
at large scale

340
00:16:34,750 --> 00:16:38,970
And when using PPO, we train value functions at large scale

341
00:16:38,970 --> 00:16:41,370
But these value functions are primarily

342
00:16:41,370 --> 00:16:43,350
used for reducing gradient variants

343
00:16:43,350 --> 00:16:45,850
and may not be accurate enough for doing

344
00:16:45,850 --> 00:16:48,770
a full actor-critic update or for selecting actions

345
00:16:48,770 --> 00:16:51,690
with a Q-learning style objective

346
00:16:51,690 --> 00:16:55,290
And so understanding whether we can scale that value

347
00:16:55,290 --> 00:16:57,730
function learning and have value functions

348
00:16:57,730 --> 00:17:00,910
be accurate enough for directly optimizing policies-- something

349
00:17:00,910 --> 00:17:02,410
that's pretty interesting to explore

350
00:17:02,410 --> 00:17:08,530
and might be important for longer horizon problems

351
00:17:08,530 --> 00:17:13,609
And then second, I mentioned that very online data collection

352
00:17:13,609 --> 00:17:17,170
can be challenging at large scale

353
00:17:17,170 --> 00:17:26,250
And as an example of this, say that you are--

354
00:17:26,250 --> 00:17:28,780
maybe say that you're like an autonomous driving company

355
00:17:28,780 --> 00:17:31,060
You want to do RL

356
00:17:31,060 --> 00:17:34,740
It's going to be hard to collect data on all of your cars

357
00:17:34,740 --> 00:17:41,140
at once, then do a policy update every 30 seconds or even

358
00:17:41,140 --> 00:17:43,060
every minute or even every hour

359
00:17:43,060 --> 00:17:46,100
It's much easier to first collect a large batch of data

360
00:17:46,100 --> 00:17:49,420
like maybe over a couple of days, maybe over a week

361
00:17:49,420 --> 00:17:51,780
then update your policy on all that data

362
00:17:51,780 --> 00:17:53,900
and repeat that process

363
00:17:53,900 --> 00:17:57,040
And so I refer to that sort of problem statement as a batch

364
00:17:57,040 --> 00:17:58,660
online setting, where you're collecting

365
00:17:58,660 --> 00:18:02,380
a batch of online data with one policy, then doing an update

366
00:18:02,380 --> 00:18:04,600
and repeating

367
00:18:04,600 --> 00:18:07,880
This more batch data collection setting

368
00:18:07,880 --> 00:18:09,380
might have challenges that are a bit

369
00:18:09,380 --> 00:18:13,190
different from your typical online reinforcement learning

370
00:18:13,190 --> 00:18:16,900
setting

371
00:18:16,900 --> 00:18:19,880
And so this sort of data collection

372
00:18:19,880 --> 00:18:20,960
should be quite practical

373
00:18:20,960 --> 00:18:23,040
You could also do this potentially asynchronously

374
00:18:23,040 --> 00:18:25,100
where you're collecting data asynchronously

375
00:18:25,100 --> 00:18:26,840
from making updates

376
00:18:26,840 --> 00:18:30,160
And at large scale, this sort of asynchronous updates

377
00:18:30,160 --> 00:18:34,630
can actually potentially also be more practical

378
00:18:34,630 --> 00:18:36,800
And there may be new considerations on the algorithm

379
00:18:36,800 --> 00:18:37,720
side

380
00:18:37,720 --> 00:18:40,380
So for example, in one recent work by Dong et al

381
00:18:40,380 --> 00:18:42,520
they found that using more expressive policies

382
00:18:42,520 --> 00:18:44,880
Like, diffusion policies was really, really important

383
00:18:44,880 --> 00:18:46,600
for effective reinforcement learning

384
00:18:46,600 --> 00:18:48,350
in this batch online setting

385
00:18:48,350 --> 00:18:50,520
And one explanation for that is if you're collecting

386
00:18:50,520 --> 00:18:51,820
a large batch of data

387
00:18:51,820 --> 00:18:55,080
Having diverse actions be represented

388
00:18:55,080 --> 00:18:57,880
could naturally be pretty important

389
00:18:57,880 --> 00:19:00,220
Because in the fully online setting

390
00:19:00,220 --> 00:19:02,230
if you have a more deterministic policy

391
00:19:02,230 --> 00:19:03,860
if you're going to be making an update

392
00:19:03,860 --> 00:19:06,760
maybe it's OK to be more deterministic because you're

393
00:19:06,760 --> 00:19:09,170
going to change your policy really, really soon anyway

394
00:19:09,170 --> 00:19:10,960
And so you'll already get more diverse data

395
00:19:10,960 --> 00:19:13,640
just from changing your policy so frequently

396
00:19:13,640 --> 00:19:15,720
Whereas in the more batch setting

397
00:19:15,720 --> 00:19:18,200
you don't have the luxury of changing your policy so

398
00:19:18,200 --> 00:19:18,880
frequently

399
00:19:18,880 --> 00:19:21,760
And having a more diverse distribution

400
00:19:21,760 --> 00:19:24,420
might be quite helpful

401
00:19:24,420 --> 00:19:27,730
So DPO-- there are algorithms that are operating much more

402
00:19:27,730 --> 00:19:29,170
offline

403
00:19:29,170 --> 00:19:31,410
And so there is success there

404
00:19:31,410 --> 00:19:33,210
The one downside of that is those problems

405
00:19:33,210 --> 00:19:34,810
are shorter horizon

406
00:19:34,810 --> 00:19:38,250
And so can we do long horizons and with less online data

407
00:19:38,250 --> 00:19:40,090
So yeah, things like offline RL are also

408
00:19:40,090 --> 00:19:43,930
going to be more scalable as well

409
00:19:43,930 --> 00:19:46,230
Let's talk a bit about deployment and evaluation

410
00:19:46,230 --> 00:19:52,530
So one problem I think that is quite important

411
00:19:52,530 --> 00:19:54,450
with these models is thinking about safety

412
00:19:54,450 --> 00:19:58,070
and how to apply these models in more safety critical domains

413
00:19:58,070 --> 00:20:00,730
That could be domains like medicine, driving, mental health

414
00:20:00,730 --> 00:20:03,810
counseling, legal and political discourse, and so on

415
00:20:03,810 --> 00:20:06,690
No shortage of domains that have challenges

416
00:20:06,690 --> 00:20:09,010
that could lead to safety critical situations when

417
00:20:09,010 --> 00:20:11,530
you make mistakes

418
00:20:11,530 --> 00:20:14,130
Now, historically, this sort of problem

419
00:20:14,130 --> 00:20:16,690
has been approached by trying to formally verify

420
00:20:16,690 --> 00:20:18,770
the behavior of the model or provide some sort

421
00:20:18,770 --> 00:20:21,330
of probabilistic guarantees

422
00:20:21,330 --> 00:20:24,230
But generally, oftentimes, these methods

423
00:20:24,230 --> 00:20:27,150
are going to make assumptions that don't hold up

424
00:20:27,150 --> 00:20:28,890
under real-world circumstances

425
00:20:28,890 --> 00:20:32,590
And so even if you can verify it in a model

426
00:20:32,590 --> 00:20:38,270
those verifications often won't hold at larger scales

427
00:20:38,270 --> 00:20:41,350
And generally, it's impossible to fully guarantee safety

428
00:20:41,350 --> 00:20:43,310
in the countless number of scenarios

429
00:20:43,310 --> 00:20:46,110
that you can encounter in the real world

430
00:20:46,110 --> 00:20:48,710
And we find that even humans make mistakes, even

431
00:20:48,710 --> 00:20:50,130
in safety critical settings

432
00:20:50,130 --> 00:20:53,690
So human drivers make mistakes, surgeons, pilots make mistakes

433
00:20:53,690 --> 00:20:57,230
politicians make mistakes, and so forth

434
00:20:57,230 --> 00:20:59,530
So we can't guarantee safety

435
00:20:59,530 --> 00:21:03,790
But we could at least try to think about handling safety

436
00:21:03,790 --> 00:21:07,310
critical scenarios effectively

437
00:21:07,310 --> 00:21:12,550
And arguably, if you were to think about safety

438
00:21:12,550 --> 00:21:14,590
there might be some constraints that we can apply

439
00:21:14,590 --> 00:21:16,430
to the model in some scenarios

440
00:21:16,430 --> 00:21:20,010
But in general, safety is an open-world problem

441
00:21:20,010 --> 00:21:22,200
You need to handle all sorts of different ways

442
00:21:22,200 --> 00:21:26,000
in which your model could do something that's unsafe

443
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,440
And arguably, large-scale machine learning

444
00:21:28,440 --> 00:21:30,760
has been the most successful way to handle

445
00:21:30,760 --> 00:21:33,240
open-world circumstances

446
00:21:33,240 --> 00:21:36,330
And so as a result of this, it seems like perhaps we

447
00:21:36,330 --> 00:21:38,120
should try to collect large amounts of data

448
00:21:38,120 --> 00:21:42,720
that would teach our model what is safe and what is unsafe

449
00:21:42,720 --> 00:21:45,120
Now, at the same time, this is really unsatisfying

450
00:21:45,120 --> 00:21:48,800
This means that we actually have to somehow collect a lot of data

451
00:21:48,800 --> 00:21:50,660
under unsafe circumstances

452
00:21:50,660 --> 00:21:56,780
And it also can have some really real and negative ramifications

453
00:21:56,780 --> 00:22:00,440
So as an example, there was news articles

454
00:22:00,440 --> 00:22:05,240
that talked about how tech companies are basically

455
00:22:05,240 --> 00:22:10,800
trying to do content moderation or label data that

456
00:22:10,800 --> 00:22:13,880
is traumatic or toxic

457
00:22:13,880 --> 00:22:17,160
And labeling that kind of data can even

458
00:22:17,160 --> 00:22:20,180
cause humans all sorts of mental health challenges

459
00:22:20,180 --> 00:22:23,900
and has a lot of ethical considerations

460
00:22:23,900 --> 00:22:28,100
And so this approach isn't satisfying for handling

461
00:22:28,100 --> 00:22:32,180
this sort of safety critical problem

462
00:22:32,180 --> 00:22:34,660
And so can we develop methods that

463
00:22:34,660 --> 00:22:37,120
can learn what's unsafe without expansive data

464
00:22:37,120 --> 00:22:38,880
without expansive human labeling

465
00:22:38,880 --> 00:22:40,660
without expansive data of incidents

466
00:22:40,660 --> 00:22:44,300
that happen themselves

467
00:22:44,300 --> 00:22:46,820
Maybe we could use synthetic data for this

468
00:22:46,820 --> 00:22:49,300
Maybe we can use some sort of prior knowledge

469
00:22:49,300 --> 00:22:51,020
This is an open challenge and would

470
00:22:51,020 --> 00:22:55,020
have a lot of benefits in terms of the safety of these systems

471
00:22:55,020 --> 00:23:00,140
and the safety of training these systems

472
00:23:00,140 --> 00:23:04,360
And then separate from that, as these models are improving

473
00:23:04,360 --> 00:23:07,620
we also want them to remain safe during the exploration process

474
00:23:07,620 --> 00:23:10,780
and during the learning process

475
00:23:10,780 --> 00:23:14,140
And this is something that humans are actually

476
00:23:14,140 --> 00:23:16,670
pretty good at doing

477
00:23:16,670 --> 00:23:22,650
And as a silly example, maybe a couple years ago

478
00:23:22,650 --> 00:23:25,110
I taught myself how to ride my bike without holding on

479
00:23:25,110 --> 00:23:26,390
to the handlebars

480
00:23:26,390 --> 00:23:30,750
And I did so without ever falling off my bike

481
00:23:30,750 --> 00:23:35,450
And I'm not saying that to brag

482
00:23:35,450 --> 00:23:36,710
It's not that hard

483
00:23:36,710 --> 00:23:39,510
It took time, took effort

484
00:23:39,510 --> 00:23:41,590
But I think it's really fascinating to think about

485
00:23:41,590 --> 00:23:44,470
how would you get a robot to, for example, learn

486
00:23:44,470 --> 00:23:47,910
how to do a new behavior like that without ever falling

487
00:23:47,910 --> 00:23:50,370
or without ever causing damage to themselves

488
00:23:50,370 --> 00:23:51,950
And it means that you're fundamentally

489
00:23:51,950 --> 00:23:53,850
exploring a new part of the state space

490
00:23:53,850 --> 00:23:57,070
while also understanding what might be more risky and less

491
00:23:57,070 --> 00:24:00,870
risky and thinking about a curriculum over how

492
00:24:00,870 --> 00:24:05,380
to accomplish that sort of objective

493
00:24:05,380 --> 00:24:08,710
So separate from safety, although related

494
00:24:08,710 --> 00:24:13,050
is that these models do, even in non-safety critical scenarios

495
00:24:13,050 --> 00:24:14,350
make mistakes

496
00:24:14,350 --> 00:24:18,150
Sometimes, these mistakes are called hallucinations

497
00:24:18,150 --> 00:24:19,730
Probably, many of you have encountered

498
00:24:19,730 --> 00:24:21,690
these kinds of mistakes before

499
00:24:21,690 --> 00:24:23,650
I'll provide one example here, where

500
00:24:23,650 --> 00:24:27,410
I was asking it to tell me what the weight of the sugar

501
00:24:27,410 --> 00:24:29,710
on the bowl is in this image

502
00:24:29,710 --> 00:24:32,290
And it confidently told me that the digital scale on the image

503
00:24:32,290 --> 00:24:34,450
reads 108.2 grams

504
00:24:34,450 --> 00:24:36,770
That is the weight of the sugar currently in the bowl

505
00:24:36,770 --> 00:24:38,220
This answer is incorrect

506
00:24:38,220 --> 00:24:40,550
It's actually a bit different from what the scale shows

507
00:24:40,550 --> 00:24:41,970
although it's also very challenging

508
00:24:41,970 --> 00:24:43,470
to read what the scale shows because

509
00:24:43,470 --> 00:24:47,410
of the glare in the image

510
00:24:47,410 --> 00:24:51,770
And oftentimes, in language models

511
00:24:51,770 --> 00:24:56,690
humans are the recipient of the predictions of these models

512
00:24:56,690 --> 00:24:58,850
And this means that oftentimes mistakes

513
00:24:58,850 --> 00:25:02,650
are fairly manageable because a human can discern whether or not

514
00:25:02,650 --> 00:25:08,090
the model was saying the right thing or the wrong thing

515
00:25:08,090 --> 00:25:10,410
But the interface between these humans and these models

516
00:25:10,410 --> 00:25:13,100
is not well optimized

517
00:25:13,100 --> 00:25:18,580
And as an example of this, there were multiple studies

518
00:25:18,580 --> 00:25:23,380
that have looked at how AI systems work with doctors

519
00:25:23,380 --> 00:25:26,220
And in one study, it found that when

520
00:25:26,220 --> 00:25:28,260
the AI was working independently

521
00:25:28,260 --> 00:25:31,980
it was able to achieve 92% accuracy

522
00:25:31,980 --> 00:25:34,860
When physicians were using AI assistants

523
00:25:34,860 --> 00:25:38,420
they only had 76% accuracy

524
00:25:38,420 --> 00:25:41,480
And that was barely better than when humans were acting alone

525
00:25:41,480 --> 00:25:44,220
which was 74% accuracy

526
00:25:44,220 --> 00:25:46,840
And it seems like for these systems to be more effective

527
00:25:46,840 --> 00:25:48,940
we should take into account the fact that they'll

528
00:25:48,940 --> 00:25:52,900
be working with humans with actually making decisions

529
00:25:52,900 --> 00:25:56,180
at the end of the day

530
00:25:56,180 --> 00:25:58,710
Like, 92 sounds great compared to 74

531
00:25:58,710 --> 00:26:00,380
But if it is going to be a system that's

532
00:26:00,380 --> 00:26:06,940
interacting with a person, 76 doesn't sound quite so great

533
00:26:06,940 --> 00:26:10,020
So can we optimize for better human AI systems

534
00:26:10,020 --> 00:26:13,000
And as part of this, maybe one way to achieve this

535
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:16,380
was if models could better estimate uncertainty and better

536
00:26:16,380 --> 00:26:17,800
estimate when they might be giving

537
00:26:17,800 --> 00:26:19,420
the right answer versus when they might

538
00:26:19,420 --> 00:26:22,760
be giving the wrong answer

539
00:26:22,760 --> 00:26:25,400
And there's research that shows that if you take a pre-trained

540
00:26:25,400 --> 00:26:29,280
model and plot the confidence of the model versus the accuracy

541
00:26:29,280 --> 00:26:30,040
of the model--

542
00:26:30,040 --> 00:26:31,960
so the confidence is the predicted probability

543
00:26:31,960 --> 00:26:33,680
of the answer that it gives- and this

544
00:26:33,680 --> 00:26:37,700
is the actual accuracy of those answers on average

545
00:26:37,700 --> 00:26:39,700
you see that it's actually very well calibrated

546
00:26:39,700 --> 00:26:42,160
So that confidence actually reflects its accuracy

547
00:26:42,160 --> 00:26:43,880
really well

548
00:26:43,880 --> 00:26:46,420
But then after post-training with reinforcement learning

549
00:26:46,420 --> 00:26:52,400
we actually see that the model becomes far less calibrated

550
00:26:52,400 --> 00:26:55,600
And so maybe reinforcement learning

551
00:26:55,600 --> 00:26:57,060
while it's making the model better

552
00:26:57,060 --> 00:27:00,180
is actually deteriorating some of the calibration

553
00:27:00,180 --> 00:27:05,080
that these models might have at the beginning

554
00:27:05,080 --> 00:27:07,200
Perhaps one direction, there was some research

555
00:27:07,200 --> 00:27:11,030
by Tian et al. that showed that if you verbalize the probability

556
00:27:11,030 --> 00:27:14,010
rather than using the probability like the logits

557
00:27:14,010 --> 00:27:17,630
in the model and encourage it to provide multiple guesses

558
00:27:17,630 --> 00:27:22,210
then you can get more calibrated model performance after RLHF

559
00:27:22,210 --> 00:27:24,530
compared to the RLHF model

560
00:27:24,530 --> 00:27:26,430
So maybe that's one possible direction

561
00:27:26,430 --> 00:27:27,810
Even then, you need to figure out

562
00:27:27,810 --> 00:27:29,390
how this would interface with a human

563
00:27:29,390 --> 00:27:32,930
and then evaluate how it does when the human and model are

564
00:27:32,930 --> 00:27:35,360
working together

565
00:27:35,360 --> 00:27:37,570
But yeah, this is, I think, an important open problem

566
00:27:37,570 --> 00:27:40,970
for thinking about how humans and AI

567
00:27:40,970 --> 00:27:44,210
systems are working together

568
00:27:44,210 --> 00:27:47,090
And then even separate from that, a lot of AI systems

569
00:27:47,090 --> 00:27:50,380
are not going to have a human there at the end of the day that

570
00:27:50,380 --> 00:27:51,590
are checking all the answers

571
00:27:51,590 --> 00:27:53,510
And this is true in autonomous driving

572
00:27:53,510 --> 00:27:55,970
where a human isn't going to be always holding the steering

573
00:27:55,970 --> 00:27:56,470
wheel

574
00:27:56,470 --> 00:27:58,730
This is true in robotics, where maybe you

575
00:27:58,730 --> 00:28:00,310
will be working with a human

576
00:28:00,310 --> 00:28:02,010
But probably, many of the decisions

577
00:28:02,010 --> 00:28:05,010
won't be checked by a human compared to many of the language

578
00:28:05,010 --> 00:28:06,060
model use cases

579
00:28:06,060 --> 00:28:08,310
And even in language models, there's lots of use cases

580
00:28:08,310 --> 00:28:13,660
where you want to just plug that in to another system

581
00:28:13,660 --> 00:28:15,010
to control some system

582
00:28:15,010 --> 00:28:18,590
Maybe web agents, for example, will be autonomously choosing

583
00:28:18,590 --> 00:28:20,390
what to do

584
00:28:20,390 --> 00:28:24,190
And so in those scenarios, can we get really high reliability

585
00:28:24,190 --> 00:28:26,970
Reinforcement learning is likely a part of the solution

586
00:28:26,970 --> 00:28:30,750
Online data is really helpful for getting high performance

587
00:28:30,750 --> 00:28:33,630
And there's promising results that reinforcement learning can

588
00:28:33,630 --> 00:28:36,070
give really high reliability

589
00:28:36,070 --> 00:28:38,070
One example by Luo et al

590
00:28:38,070 --> 00:28:41,050
showed that on fairly challenging robot tasks

591
00:28:41,050 --> 00:28:46,150
they're able to get performance where they ran the policy 100

592
00:28:46,150 --> 00:28:48,830
times and it worked 100 times

593
00:28:48,830 --> 00:28:50,310
So that's pretty cool

594
00:28:50,310 --> 00:28:52,550
But extending this to large-scale scenarios

595
00:28:52,550 --> 00:28:54,950
where you need to have really high reliability

596
00:28:54,950 --> 00:29:00,000
is quite important

597
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:06,760
And then the last thing I'll talk about is evaluation

598
00:29:06,760 --> 00:29:11,460
So in supervised learning, we can measure accuracy

599
00:29:11,460 --> 00:29:13,260
And we can use held out validation sets

600
00:29:13,260 --> 00:29:15,010
And this is kind of like heaven because we

601
00:29:15,010 --> 00:29:17,760
can get a very nice measure offline

602
00:29:17,760 --> 00:29:20,560
of how good our model is

603
00:29:20,560 --> 00:29:22,460
But in reinforcement learning in general

604
00:29:22,460 --> 00:29:25,220
there aren't any reliable offline metrics

605
00:29:25,220 --> 00:29:27,020
And to evaluate performance, typically

606
00:29:27,020 --> 00:29:29,640
at the end of the day, you just need to run your policy

607
00:29:29,640 --> 00:29:32,120
roll it out in whatever environment you're in

608
00:29:32,120 --> 00:29:37,080
and measure the reward or measure the performance

609
00:29:37,080 --> 00:29:43,000
And the reason for this is that this data collected so far

610
00:29:43,000 --> 00:29:46,640
under whatever policy, differs from the data that was collected

611
00:29:46,640 --> 00:29:48,480
by the learned policy

612
00:29:48,480 --> 00:29:52,920
And so it's really hard to evaluate the policy on states

613
00:29:52,920 --> 00:29:54,680
that the policy is going to visit

614
00:29:54,680 --> 00:29:56,520
when you don't have those set of states

615
00:29:56,520 --> 00:30:01,320
that the policy will necessarily visit

616
00:30:01,320 --> 00:30:05,170
Now, to make matters worse, if we have a generalized system

617
00:30:05,170 --> 00:30:09,530
that is intended not just to do one task but to do many tasks

618
00:30:09,530 --> 00:30:11,490
then we need to evaluate our policy

619
00:30:11,490 --> 00:30:15,030
not just in one circumstance but in all sorts of circumstances

620
00:30:15,030 --> 00:30:16,610
And this means that we need to collect

621
00:30:16,610 --> 00:30:21,210
large amounts of online data and evaluate all

622
00:30:21,210 --> 00:30:22,970
of those different rollouts

623
00:30:22,970 --> 00:30:24,890
And this makes it really hard to decide first

624
00:30:24,890 --> 00:30:28,370
if the policy is good enough to deploy or even just

625
00:30:28,370 --> 00:30:32,650
the simpler problem of picking which of your different models

626
00:30:32,650 --> 00:30:38,250
is the best model and choosing hyperparameters, for example

627
00:30:38,250 --> 00:30:41,890
As a real-world circumstance of this problem

628
00:30:41,890 --> 00:30:45,450
about a month ago, OpenAI released

629
00:30:45,450 --> 00:30:51,730
a ChatGPT model, a new version of GPT-4o, I think

630
00:30:51,730 --> 00:30:55,730
And if you asked it, how many people

631
00:30:55,730 --> 00:30:58,370
am I better than at thinking

632
00:30:58,370 --> 00:31:00,350
it said, if I had to put a number on it

633
00:31:00,350 --> 00:31:03,350
I'd estimate that you're easily in the 130 to 145

634
00:31:03,350 --> 00:31:08,790
range of IQ, which would put you above about 98% to 99.7%

635
00:31:08,790 --> 00:31:10,320
of people in raw thinking ability

636
00:31:10,320 --> 00:31:12,070
But honestly, comparing you to most people

637
00:31:12,070 --> 00:31:16,070
almost insults the quality of mind you're aiming to develop

638
00:31:16,070 --> 00:31:19,470
So maybe this isn't what you're kind of expecting or looking

639
00:31:19,470 --> 00:31:21,850
for in your model

640
00:31:21,850 --> 00:31:23,790
And OpenAI actually ended up needing

641
00:31:23,790 --> 00:31:27,950
to rollback this update that made the bot excessively

642
00:31:27,950 --> 00:31:30,890
flattering to users

643
00:31:30,890 --> 00:31:32,310
This gets at the challenge of it's

644
00:31:32,310 --> 00:31:36,190
really hard to know if the model that you're developing is good

645
00:31:36,190 --> 00:31:38,570
enough, is better than your previous model, and so forth

646
00:31:38,570 --> 00:31:43,750
It's really hard to evaluate these systems

647
00:31:43,750 --> 00:31:46,110
A couple specific open questions

648
00:31:46,110 --> 00:31:49,190
First, can we develop offline metrics that

649
00:31:49,190 --> 00:31:51,670
can at least rule out bad models

650
00:31:51,670 --> 00:31:53,630
let alone estimate performance

651
00:31:53,630 --> 00:31:57,380
Estimating performance would be the dream

652
00:31:57,380 --> 00:31:59,590
But at the very least, maybe we could rule out models

653
00:31:59,590 --> 00:32:03,800
that we shouldn't deploy offline

654
00:32:03,800 --> 00:32:06,500
And then even in terms of online policy evaluation

655
00:32:06,500 --> 00:32:09,620
if we need to evaluate a policy in all sorts of circumstances

656
00:32:09,620 --> 00:32:13,800
can we figure out how best to select representative scenarios

657
00:32:13,800 --> 00:32:16,280
that will allow us to measure generalist

658
00:32:16,280 --> 00:32:18,800
performance without having to evaluate

659
00:32:18,800 --> 00:32:23,480
every single possible scenario

660
00:32:23,480 --> 00:32:27,700
Now, that was all I had for frontiers and open problems

661
00:32:27,700 --> 00:32:30,880
And I guess one thing I want to convey

662
00:32:30,880 --> 00:32:33,050
is that now that you've taken the class

663
00:32:33,050 --> 00:32:34,840
you should be really well equipped to start

664
00:32:34,840 --> 00:32:36,250
tackling these challenges

665
00:32:36,250 --> 00:32:38,540
You don't know everything about reinforcement learning

666
00:32:38,540 --> 00:32:39,060
of course

667
00:32:39,060 --> 00:32:43,120
But you should know a lot of the fundamentals that

668
00:32:43,120 --> 00:32:45,220
will allow you to go read research papers

669
00:32:45,220 --> 00:32:48,800
understand where the state of the art is

670
00:32:48,800 --> 00:32:51,520
and build on the state of the art

671
00:32:51,520 --> 00:32:54,760
So your question is, why does this happen basically

672
00:32:54,760 --> 00:32:56,660
So I don't think we necessarily expect this

673
00:32:56,660 --> 00:32:59,400
But looking at this in retrospect

674
00:32:59,400 --> 00:33:02,900
I think it's reasonable to make some guesses for why

675
00:33:02,900 --> 00:33:03,680
this is happening

676
00:33:03,680 --> 00:33:06,340
And I think the predominant theory for why this is happening

677
00:33:06,340 --> 00:33:10,720
is that the objective that you're optimizing for, first

678
00:33:10,720 --> 00:33:12,220
is not exactly accuracy

679
00:33:12,220 --> 00:33:14,320
It's not exactly aligned with that

680
00:33:14,320 --> 00:33:17,660
And typically, what reinforcement

681
00:33:17,660 --> 00:33:20,060
learning with human feedback-- what it's doing oftentimes

682
00:33:20,060 --> 00:33:22,300
is actually sharpening the distribution

683
00:33:22,300 --> 00:33:24,100
So the pre-trained model, it often

684
00:33:24,100 --> 00:33:25,900
will output all sorts of stuff

685
00:33:25,900 --> 00:33:28,540
And what RL is trying to do is-- let's

686
00:33:28,540 --> 00:33:30,620
say this is the mode that we really want our model

687
00:33:30,620 --> 00:33:31,600
to be predicting

688
00:33:31,600 --> 00:33:33,390
And it's to be sharpening that distribution

689
00:33:33,390 --> 00:33:36,500
to really encourage the model to be more likely to sample one

690
00:33:36,500 --> 00:33:37,660
mode

691
00:33:37,660 --> 00:33:40,780
And that sharpening often leads to it

692
00:33:40,780 --> 00:33:44,860
being overconfident in certain circumstances, especially

693
00:33:44,860 --> 00:33:52,220
when preferences are rewarding you for being confident

694
00:33:52,220 --> 00:33:55,700
and then also, at times being correct and confident

695
00:33:55,700 --> 00:34:00,770
and sometimes being incorrect and confident

696
00:34:00,770 --> 00:34:04,670
So let's talk a little bit about how to do research

697
00:34:04,670 --> 00:34:09,110
And this will be a focus on how to do research that's

698
00:34:09,110 --> 00:34:12,350
more of an empirical flavor similar to the research

699
00:34:12,350 --> 00:34:14,830
that you do if you were doing a deep reinforcement learning

700
00:34:14,830 --> 00:34:17,110
project

701
00:34:17,110 --> 00:34:20,110
Now, before we get started, I have

702
00:34:20,110 --> 00:34:22,790
a couple different thoughts

703
00:34:22,790 --> 00:34:25,510
The first is that I'll talk a lot

704
00:34:25,510 --> 00:34:28,230
about how I go about the research process and advice

705
00:34:28,230 --> 00:34:30,630
that I give to PhD students as I see

706
00:34:30,630 --> 00:34:33,590
them working through various research projects

707
00:34:33,590 --> 00:34:35,870
But there's many different ways to do research

708
00:34:35,870 --> 00:34:38,550
And it's important and good for the community

709
00:34:38,550 --> 00:34:40,570
to do research in many different ways

710
00:34:40,570 --> 00:34:43,150
because that leads to more diverse discoveries

711
00:34:43,150 --> 00:34:46,250
And if everyone did research the exact way that I do research

712
00:34:46,250 --> 00:34:50,770
then we would probably have fewer diverse discoveries

713
00:34:50,770 --> 00:34:52,449
And so diversity is good

714
00:34:52,449 --> 00:34:57,930
And my research approach is not necessarily the best approach

715
00:34:57,930 --> 00:34:59,890
And I don't think there's any one approach that

716
00:34:59,890 --> 00:35:01,740
is the best approach

717
00:35:01,740 --> 00:35:03,450
I also felt like it could be good to give

718
00:35:03,450 --> 00:35:06,410
a little bit of a backstory, which is that I did not intend

719
00:35:06,410 --> 00:35:08,330
to have a career in research

720
00:35:08,330 --> 00:35:14,090
I was planning to go to industry and develop products

721
00:35:14,090 --> 00:35:17,970
which is what my parents did in various engineering fields

722
00:35:17,970 --> 00:35:20,430
And I felt like that was the way to have real-world impact

723
00:35:20,430 --> 00:35:25,850
I did internships in industry and eventually figured out

724
00:35:25,850 --> 00:35:28,410
that I was excited about AI

725
00:35:28,410 --> 00:35:30,490
And I was excited about algorithm development

726
00:35:30,490 --> 00:35:33,170
And it seemed like the frontier of that and algorithm

727
00:35:33,170 --> 00:35:35,730
development was really in a research environment

728
00:35:35,730 --> 00:35:38,050
rather than in an industry environment

729
00:35:38,050 --> 00:35:40,810
And a lot of the topics I was excited about

730
00:35:40,810 --> 00:35:41,970
didn't really work

731
00:35:41,970 --> 00:35:44,190
Things like robotics didn't really work

732
00:35:44,190 --> 00:35:47,330
Even today, robots often don't work very well

733
00:35:47,330 --> 00:35:50,410
And so they're really in this sort of research domain

734
00:35:50,410 --> 00:35:52,610
Also, the people that I met in industry

735
00:35:52,610 --> 00:35:54,380
that were working on the kinds of things

736
00:35:54,380 --> 00:35:56,470
that I wanted to be working on all had PhDs

737
00:35:56,470 --> 00:35:58,800
And so I was like, well, I should probably get a PhD too

738
00:35:58,800 --> 00:36:01,100
if I want to work on those things

739
00:36:01,100 --> 00:36:03,420
And then I also just found research and the ambiguity

740
00:36:03,420 --> 00:36:06,260
of research to be really kind of intellectually

741
00:36:06,260 --> 00:36:08,940
interesting and challenging

742
00:36:08,940 --> 00:36:10,740
And so that's all to say that you

743
00:36:10,740 --> 00:36:14,140
don't have to intend to have to work in research

744
00:36:14,140 --> 00:36:19,760
in order to eventually end up doing research

745
00:36:19,760 --> 00:36:21,260
And then the last thing I'll mention

746
00:36:21,260 --> 00:36:25,220
before getting into how to do research

747
00:36:25,220 --> 00:36:30,620
is just like some general realities with research

748
00:36:30,620 --> 00:36:35,500
The first is that very, very few research ideas are good

749
00:36:35,500 --> 00:36:37,620
And very, very few research ideas

750
00:36:37,620 --> 00:36:41,060
have impact, have lasting impact especially

751
00:36:41,060 --> 00:36:42,820
A lot of ideas don't work

752
00:36:42,820 --> 00:36:45,300
A lot of ideas don't work to the extent

753
00:36:45,300 --> 00:36:46,940
that they won't lead to papers

754
00:36:46,940 --> 00:36:50,060
As an example of this, here are the outcomes

755
00:36:50,060 --> 00:36:52,080
of some of my research ideas

756
00:36:52,080 --> 00:36:54,640
And here are the outcome of some more of my research ideas

757
00:36:54,640 --> 00:36:58,040
And there's a lot of failure

758
00:36:58,040 --> 00:37:03,080
And the risk and the challenge of doing research

759
00:37:03,080 --> 00:37:04,930
informs different ways--

760
00:37:04,930 --> 00:37:06,800
a lot of what I'll say has to do with dealing

761
00:37:06,800 --> 00:37:10,960
with that sort of risk and dealing with those challenges

762
00:37:10,960 --> 00:37:14,920
And then even if it does lead to a paper, a lot of papers

763
00:37:14,920 --> 00:37:18,820
won't necessarily have a lasting impact

764
00:37:18,820 --> 00:37:20,240
The second thing that I'll mention

765
00:37:20,240 --> 00:37:24,740
is that research is inherently incremental

766
00:37:24,740 --> 00:37:27,720
We are building upon the knowledge of previous research

767
00:37:27,720 --> 00:37:29,080
that's been done

768
00:37:29,080 --> 00:37:31,920
And as an example of this, AlphaFold

769
00:37:31,920 --> 00:37:37,200
arguably like a really impactful thing, very, very successful

770
00:37:37,200 --> 00:37:40,720
arguably could be considered a breakthrough

771
00:37:40,720 --> 00:37:43,460
This itself was, in many ways, incremental

772
00:37:43,460 --> 00:37:47,480
It built very closely on a large 20-year academic project called

773
00:37:47,480 --> 00:37:52,370
CASP, which was a protein structure prediction project

774
00:37:52,370 --> 00:37:55,450
that was basically kind of a competition and a data set

775
00:37:55,450 --> 00:37:58,490
for doing this sort of structure prediction and also built very

776
00:37:58,490 --> 00:38:00,470
heavily on advances in neural networks

777
00:38:00,470 --> 00:38:04,090
And so even if any one research project isn't a breakthrough

778
00:38:04,090 --> 00:38:07,210
it often plays an important role because it allows other projects

779
00:38:07,210 --> 00:38:10,170
to build on it or other projects to potentially rule out ideas

780
00:38:10,170 --> 00:38:12,140
that have been tried already

781
00:38:12,140 --> 00:38:13,850
And then the last thing that I'll mention

782
00:38:13,850 --> 00:38:17,410
is that oftentimes people think about developing

783
00:38:17,410 --> 00:38:19,350
really cool, fancy algorithms

784
00:38:19,350 --> 00:38:24,190
But in a world where scaling up is often quite important

785
00:38:24,190 --> 00:38:27,450
especially in AI research, oftentimes

786
00:38:27,450 --> 00:38:29,010
it's actually the simple ideas that

787
00:38:29,010 --> 00:38:33,910
have more impact than the fancy ideas or the complex algorithms

788
00:38:33,910 --> 00:38:36,630
And that's just because the simple ideas are the things that

789
00:38:36,630 --> 00:38:38,090
can scale, that are the things that

790
00:38:38,090 --> 00:38:41,570
are easy to be integrated into different systems

791
00:38:41,570 --> 00:38:48,450
and easy to build on top of

792
00:38:48,450 --> 00:38:50,350
With that out of the way, I'll talk

793
00:38:50,350 --> 00:38:53,550
about what to work on, how to do the work

794
00:38:53,550 --> 00:38:58,430
how to share the work, and then a couple miscellaneous topics

795
00:38:58,430 --> 00:39:02,750
And I would argue that these three things--

796
00:39:02,750 --> 00:39:05,110
picking problems to work on, doing the research

797
00:39:05,110 --> 00:39:06,550
and sharing the work--

798
00:39:06,550 --> 00:39:08,590
are all equally important

799
00:39:08,590 --> 00:39:11,670
And it's easy to think that maybe number 2 is the most

800
00:39:11,670 --> 00:39:13,020
important or maybe number 1

801
00:39:13,020 --> 00:39:15,270
But all of these are actually really, really important

802
00:39:15,270 --> 00:39:20,140
for being successful in research

803
00:39:20,140 --> 00:39:23,670
So step 1 for what to work on--

804
00:39:23,670 --> 00:39:26,550
I think that a research project needs two things

805
00:39:26,550 --> 00:39:29,310
The first is an important problem

806
00:39:29,310 --> 00:39:33,310
And the second is a plan for how to approach that problem

807
00:39:33,310 --> 00:39:36,190
And if you only have one of these, then

808
00:39:36,190 --> 00:39:39,390
usually that doesn't constitute a good project direction

809
00:39:39,390 --> 00:39:41,950
So for example, maybe one project idea

810
00:39:41,950 --> 00:39:44,790
is I want to solve climate change

811
00:39:44,790 --> 00:39:47,000
That is an important problem

812
00:39:47,000 --> 00:39:49,440
But I don't have a plan for how to approach it

813
00:39:49,440 --> 00:39:51,340
So it doesn't make a good research project

814
00:39:51,340 --> 00:39:54,880
even though it's a really important problem

815
00:39:54,880 --> 00:39:57,640
Second, maybe I have a really cool algorithm

816
00:39:57,640 --> 00:40:00,800
that at best will make a robot or a reinforcement learning

817
00:40:00,800 --> 00:40:03,800
system a little bit more successful

818
00:40:03,800 --> 00:40:07,420
This is maybe a really cool idea

819
00:40:07,420 --> 00:40:08,420
And maybe I have a plan

820
00:40:08,420 --> 00:40:09,280
Maybe I have two

821
00:40:09,280 --> 00:40:11,420
But I'm missing the important problem

822
00:40:11,420 --> 00:40:15,880
that this is actually solving

823
00:40:15,880 --> 00:40:17,500
And so for this latter part, I think

824
00:40:17,500 --> 00:40:19,040
it's really important to think about

825
00:40:19,040 --> 00:40:22,130
if the project is really, really successful

826
00:40:22,130 --> 00:40:23,460
what will the outcome look like

827
00:40:23,460 --> 00:40:24,920
Does it solve an important problem

828
00:40:24,920 --> 00:40:27,360
Or what is the upper bound of that project

829
00:40:27,360 --> 00:40:30,520
And if the upper bound is not very high, then

830
00:40:30,520 --> 00:40:35,360
usually it doesn't make for a very good project

831
00:40:35,360 --> 00:40:37,640
Step 2, if you have an important problem and a plan

832
00:40:37,640 --> 00:40:42,440
for how to approach it, are you excited about it

833
00:40:42,440 --> 00:40:44,540
Research is a lot of work

834
00:40:44,540 --> 00:40:48,720
And if it's fun, if you think it's really cool and exciting

835
00:40:48,720 --> 00:40:50,880
then you're more likely to work hard on it

836
00:40:50,880 --> 00:40:53,120
And if you're working hard on it

837
00:40:53,120 --> 00:40:56,410
you're much more likely to be successful on it

838
00:40:56,410 --> 00:40:58,660
And this is actually probably true for a lot of things

839
00:40:58,660 --> 00:41:00,140
outside of research too

840
00:41:00,140 --> 00:41:03,660
It's good to spend time on the things

841
00:41:03,660 --> 00:41:06,380
that you find exciting, that you find really cool

842
00:41:06,380 --> 00:41:08,780
Probably, if you can find some of those things--

843
00:41:08,780 --> 00:41:10,390
they may not even be just one thing

844
00:41:10,390 --> 00:41:12,260
If you can just find something that you think

845
00:41:12,260 --> 00:41:14,020
is really cool and exciting, then

846
00:41:14,020 --> 00:41:18,100
you'll probably do a lot better at it

847
00:41:18,100 --> 00:41:23,380
And then step 3, like I mentioned before, a lot of ideas

848
00:41:23,380 --> 00:41:24,460
don't work

849
00:41:24,460 --> 00:41:26,180
And so it's really good to think about

850
00:41:26,180 --> 00:41:30,340
if you are brutally honest about why your idea could fail

851
00:41:30,340 --> 00:41:33,100
to solve the problem, does the idea still

852
00:41:33,100 --> 00:41:35,540
have a good chance of working

853
00:41:35,540 --> 00:41:39,580
And if not, then probably it won't work

854
00:41:39,580 --> 00:41:43,200
And that's mostly just because of the reality of most ideas

855
00:41:43,200 --> 00:41:44,570
not actually working in research

856
00:41:44,570 --> 00:41:46,450
And it's important to be honest with yourself

857
00:41:46,450 --> 00:41:51,790
about whether this will actually solve the problem or not

858
00:41:51,790 --> 00:41:55,310
So that's my three-step process for picking problems

859
00:41:55,310 --> 00:41:57,390
Now, a couple more thoughts on this

860
00:41:57,390 --> 00:42:04,150
So research can often either start with an idea or a problem

861
00:42:04,150 --> 00:42:08,230
And I think both of these sorts of approaches to a project

862
00:42:08,230 --> 00:42:09,450
have merits

863
00:42:09,450 --> 00:42:11,730
And I've done research that's more idea-driven

864
00:42:11,730 --> 00:42:14,510
I've also done research that's more h driven

865
00:42:14,510 --> 00:42:17,830
But I tend to lean towards one myself

866
00:42:17,830 --> 00:42:20,730
And the reason is as follows

867
00:42:20,730 --> 00:42:23,190
So if you start with an idea, you

868
00:42:23,190 --> 00:42:27,230
need to find a problem that that idea solves

869
00:42:27,230 --> 00:42:29,430
And there may not exist an important problem

870
00:42:29,430 --> 00:42:31,190
that the idea solves

871
00:42:31,190 --> 00:42:33,530
It may solve a problem

872
00:42:33,530 --> 00:42:35,990
But there might be a better way to solve the problem

873
00:42:35,990 --> 00:42:38,050
And if there's a better way to solve the problem

874
00:42:38,050 --> 00:42:42,680
then that idea may not have a lot of impact

875
00:42:42,680 --> 00:42:44,900
On the contrary, if you start with a problem

876
00:42:44,900 --> 00:42:48,080
then you're trying to find the best solution to that problem

877
00:42:48,080 --> 00:42:51,560
And one downside is it may be harder to write a paper

878
00:42:51,560 --> 00:42:55,560
if the solution ends up being kind of obvious in retrospect

879
00:42:55,560 --> 00:42:57,720
But at least you're guaranteed to be working

880
00:42:57,720 --> 00:42:59,400
on an important problem

881
00:42:59,400 --> 00:43:01,040
And you find a solution

882
00:43:01,040 --> 00:43:02,820
You'll still be happy even if it ends up

883
00:43:02,820 --> 00:43:04,440
being a little bit harder to figure out

884
00:43:04,440 --> 00:43:07,480
how to write a paper about it

885
00:43:07,480 --> 00:43:10,440
Another way to put this is that with idea-driven research

886
00:43:10,440 --> 00:43:12,880
your goal is to get the idea to work

887
00:43:12,880 --> 00:43:15,880
Whereas in the second case, your goal is to solve the problem

888
00:43:15,880 --> 00:43:19,560
And the second camp seems like a much more comfortable place

889
00:43:19,560 --> 00:43:23,050
to be because it's possible that the idea just

890
00:43:23,050 --> 00:43:23,920
doesn't want to work

891
00:43:23,920 --> 00:43:28,120
Or it's not going to work at the end of the day

892
00:43:28,120 --> 00:43:31,640
Another note here is that if you're thinking about a problem

893
00:43:31,640 --> 00:43:34,560
that you want to solve, it's really important

894
00:43:34,560 --> 00:43:37,240
to get to the bottleneck of that problem

895
00:43:37,240 --> 00:43:40,000
So if the problem is bottlenecked on data

896
00:43:40,000 --> 00:43:41,980
for example, and you start exploring

897
00:43:41,980 --> 00:43:45,500
new algorithms separate from the data for how to solve it

898
00:43:45,500 --> 00:43:48,740
then you actually probably won't see performance gains

899
00:43:48,740 --> 00:43:53,100
from working on methods if it's bottlenecked by data

900
00:43:53,100 --> 00:43:55,140
And most, maybe you'll see a couple percentage

901
00:43:55,140 --> 00:43:57,620
points because, really, the big performance gains are

902
00:43:57,620 --> 00:43:59,460
going to be on the data side

903
00:43:59,460 --> 00:44:00,900
And so if you pick the things that

904
00:44:00,900 --> 00:44:03,460
are bottlenecks and the things that are actually preventing

905
00:44:03,460 --> 00:44:05,980
that thing from working, then you're

906
00:44:05,980 --> 00:44:08,280
more likely to be able to make progress

907
00:44:08,280 --> 00:44:11,340
And so I think a lot of problem-driven research

908
00:44:11,340 --> 00:44:13,900
ends up thinking about what is the bottleneck for solving

909
00:44:13,900 --> 00:44:15,500
this problem

910
00:44:15,500 --> 00:44:17,900
And this can also be even in terms of much longer term

911
00:44:17,900 --> 00:44:18,400
thinking

912
00:44:18,400 --> 00:44:27,780
You can think about, I want to develop an LLM that can solve--

913
00:44:27,780 --> 00:44:31,120
it can prove something that humans have never proved before

914
00:44:31,120 --> 00:44:33,020
like P versus NP or something

915
00:44:33,020 --> 00:44:36,700
Or I want to get a robot that can make a bowl of cereal

916
00:44:36,700 --> 00:44:38,790
in a new kitchen

917
00:44:38,790 --> 00:44:43,670
Those are things that you can't solve in any individual research

918
00:44:43,670 --> 00:44:44,570
project

919
00:44:44,570 --> 00:44:46,030
But you can think about, OK, what's

920
00:44:46,030 --> 00:44:47,410
the bottleneck for getting there

921
00:44:47,410 --> 00:44:49,590
And can I work on the part of that problem

922
00:44:49,590 --> 00:44:52,150
in my next project that will make progress

923
00:44:52,150 --> 00:44:54,390
on that larger goal

924
00:44:54,390 --> 00:44:56,750
A couple more things

925
00:44:56,750 --> 00:44:59,590
For this next thing, I want to tell a little bit of a story

926
00:44:59,590 --> 00:45:03,110
So in the second year of my PhD, I did an internship

927
00:45:03,110 --> 00:45:04,630
And I did an internship at Google

928
00:45:04,630 --> 00:45:06,570
where they had all these robots

929
00:45:06,570 --> 00:45:09,870
And I wanted to use the robots to do something cool

930
00:45:09,870 --> 00:45:12,910
And my project idea was to build a predictive model

931
00:45:12,910 --> 00:45:15,010
actually a video generation model

932
00:45:15,010 --> 00:45:20,310
and use it to learn skills across the robots

933
00:45:20,310 --> 00:45:21,950
And then there was a problem

934
00:45:21,950 --> 00:45:25,150
Most research projects have problems that you find

935
00:45:25,150 --> 00:45:28,590
And in particular, I discovered that video generation models

936
00:45:28,590 --> 00:45:30,590
were really bad

937
00:45:30,590 --> 00:45:34,430
So here was a video that I collected on the robot

938
00:45:34,430 --> 00:45:39,650
And here was a prediction from arguably the state of the art

939
00:45:39,650 --> 00:45:42,010
video generation model

940
00:45:42,010 --> 00:45:44,570
And you can see that it is erasing

941
00:45:44,570 --> 00:45:47,770
all the objects in the video and is barely capturing

942
00:45:47,770 --> 00:45:49,170
the motion of the arm

943
00:45:49,170 --> 00:45:52,770
Doesn't even capture the background correctly

944
00:45:52,770 --> 00:45:55,610
And so this means that I wasn't able to actually use

945
00:45:55,610 --> 00:45:57,930
these kinds of models to actually get the robot

946
00:45:57,930 --> 00:46:01,130
to do something better

947
00:46:01,130 --> 00:46:03,810
Now, at the time, I kind of viewed myself

948
00:46:03,810 --> 00:46:06,570
as a machine learning and robotics person and not really

949
00:46:06,570 --> 00:46:08,250
a computer vision person, not a person

950
00:46:08,250 --> 00:46:10,530
who works on generative models

951
00:46:10,530 --> 00:46:13,440
If I kind of felt like I should only work on machine learning

952
00:46:13,440 --> 00:46:15,730
and robotics, I might have to table this and figure out

953
00:46:15,730 --> 00:46:17,490
a different project to work on

954
00:46:17,490 --> 00:46:22,330
But instead, I decided to work on trying to develop a better

955
00:46:22,330 --> 00:46:24,210
video generation model

956
00:46:24,210 --> 00:46:26,140
Now, arguably, I wasn't necessarily

957
00:46:26,140 --> 00:46:27,270
the best person to do this

958
00:46:27,270 --> 00:46:29,230
I had never built a generative model before

959
00:46:29,230 --> 00:46:30,730
I didn't really know that much about

960
00:46:30,730 --> 00:46:32,210
architectures for doing so

961
00:46:32,210 --> 00:46:34,240
But not a lot of people were working on it

962
00:46:34,240 --> 00:46:35,860
at the time because they didn't realize

963
00:46:35,860 --> 00:46:37,620
how bad these models were

964
00:46:37,620 --> 00:46:40,580
And then the resulting paper ended up being the basis

965
00:46:40,580 --> 00:46:44,500
for work in my job talk, now has around 1,300 citations

966
00:46:44,500 --> 00:46:47,100
And it arguably informed the community

967
00:46:47,100 --> 00:46:49,220
that it's kind of an interesting problem to study

968
00:46:49,220 --> 00:46:53,340
and led to much better video generation models than the one

969
00:46:53,340 --> 00:46:55,780
that I was able to develop

970
00:46:55,780 --> 00:46:58,450
And so I guess the kind of lesson

971
00:46:58,450 --> 00:46:59,820
that I'm trying to convey here is

972
00:46:59,820 --> 00:47:03,440
that it's good to not box yourself into an area and say

973
00:47:03,440 --> 00:47:06,300
I'm only going to work on this particular area

974
00:47:06,300 --> 00:47:09,940
And the reason for that is if you cross topic boundaries

975
00:47:09,940 --> 00:47:12,030
it's going to inform new problems

976
00:47:12,030 --> 00:47:13,740
Because if you're trying to use something

977
00:47:13,740 --> 00:47:18,360
that is a problem for advancing the topic you want to work on

978
00:47:18,360 --> 00:47:19,940
then that kind of informs that there's

979
00:47:19,940 --> 00:47:21,200
a problem with that existing thing

980
00:47:21,200 --> 00:47:22,700
And oftentimes, people in that field

981
00:47:22,700 --> 00:47:24,900
don't know that it's a problem

982
00:47:24,900 --> 00:47:26,540
And then it also brings new ideas

983
00:47:26,540 --> 00:47:30,430
to that other topic or domain as well

984
00:47:30,430 --> 00:47:32,680
And so I see a lot of people kind of boxing themselves

985
00:47:32,680 --> 00:47:33,260
into areas

986
00:47:33,260 --> 00:47:36,520
But I think that actually crossing topic boundaries

987
00:47:36,520 --> 00:47:41,570
ends up leading to a lot of new insights and innovations

988
00:47:41,570 --> 00:47:43,280
And then the last thing that I'll mention

989
00:47:43,280 --> 00:47:45,940
is don't be a perfectionist

990
00:47:45,940 --> 00:47:48,760
So I've seen people feel like they want to pick

991
00:47:48,760 --> 00:47:50,760
the perfect project to work on

992
00:47:50,760 --> 00:47:53,120
But you never know what the impact of a project

993
00:47:53,120 --> 00:47:54,560
will be at the outset

994
00:47:54,560 --> 00:47:56,680
Otherwise, it wouldn't be research

995
00:47:56,680 --> 00:47:59,360
And so at some point, you need to just start

996
00:47:59,360 --> 00:48:04,400
trying different ideas, start getting going on a project

997
00:48:04,400 --> 00:48:09,680
without trying to find the perfect project

998
00:48:09,680 --> 00:48:13,480
So once you have a project idea or a project direction

999
00:48:13,480 --> 00:48:16,200
a key consideration from there is handling risk

1000
00:48:16,200 --> 00:48:21,120
So we've talked about how lots of research ideas don't work

1001
00:48:21,120 --> 00:48:24,080
How do we deal with that

1002
00:48:24,080 --> 00:48:27,060
So there's a lot of tricks for dealing with the risk

1003
00:48:27,060 --> 00:48:29,880
And not all of them are going to apply to every single project

1004
00:48:29,880 --> 00:48:31,250
But these are tricks that I think

1005
00:48:31,250 --> 00:48:36,850
you should be able to draw upon to handle the risk in research

1006
00:48:36,850 --> 00:48:40,010
So the first is, wherever possible

1007
00:48:40,010 --> 00:48:42,710
it's good to front load the risk in the project

1008
00:48:42,710 --> 00:48:46,530
Think about, what is the reason why this might not succeed

1009
00:48:46,530 --> 00:48:48,630
And before developing infrastructure

1010
00:48:48,630 --> 00:48:53,290
before coding up the entire algorithm and so forth

1011
00:48:53,290 --> 00:48:56,890
if you can formulate and run simple experiments that

1012
00:48:56,890 --> 00:48:59,410
test the core unknowns that you have

1013
00:48:59,410 --> 00:49:03,890
then you can bring the risk to the beginning of the project

1014
00:49:03,890 --> 00:49:05,970
And this can be really uncomfortable

1015
00:49:05,970 --> 00:49:09,070
Because oftentimes, building the infrastructure, coding

1016
00:49:09,070 --> 00:49:11,190
the algorithm, whatever, that's like the easy part

1017
00:49:11,190 --> 00:49:14,250
That's the part where you can seemingly make some progress

1018
00:49:14,250 --> 00:49:15,610
on the project

1019
00:49:15,610 --> 00:49:17,930
But if you do all that and then, at the end

1020
00:49:17,930 --> 00:49:21,170
realize that the core unknown actually ended

1021
00:49:21,170 --> 00:49:24,610
up not working out, then you wasted all of that time--

1022
00:49:24,610 --> 00:49:26,530
even though it seemingly seemed like progress

1023
00:49:26,530 --> 00:49:29,550
you wasted all that time on that idea

1024
00:49:29,550 --> 00:49:32,750
that wasn't going to pan out at the end of the day

1025
00:49:32,750 --> 00:49:35,150
And so instead, if you can frontload that risk

1026
00:49:35,150 --> 00:49:37,070
and frontload the unknowns, run experiments

1027
00:49:37,070 --> 00:49:41,350
to test those unknowns, then you can make sure that--

1028
00:49:41,350 --> 00:49:44,790
if this is applicable, you can do that first

1029
00:49:44,790 --> 00:49:46,870
and then build out the infrastructure

1030
00:49:46,870 --> 00:49:48,550
or build out the algorithm completely

1031
00:49:48,550 --> 00:49:54,190
and run all the experiments after doing that initial test

1032
00:49:54,190 --> 00:49:59,350
Along the same lines, thinking through how

1033
00:49:59,350 --> 00:50:02,590
to test ideas in the simplest possible way or the fastest

1034
00:50:02,590 --> 00:50:06,230
possible way takes some skill and takes some thinking

1035
00:50:06,230 --> 00:50:12,370
It's often not obvious how to test a particular hypothesis

1036
00:50:12,370 --> 00:50:15,190
So separate from trying to frontload risk and frontload

1037
00:50:15,190 --> 00:50:20,310
experiments is simply just iterating a lot

1038
00:50:20,310 --> 00:50:25,910
So you have to get lucky to stumble upon a good idea

1039
00:50:25,910 --> 00:50:27,880
But you can create luck for yourself

1040
00:50:27,880 --> 00:50:30,200
just by trying out lots of ideas

1041
00:50:30,200 --> 00:50:32,680
by iterating on lots of different ideas

1042
00:50:32,680 --> 00:50:36,720
And if you try out an idea and the targeted experiment doesn't

1043
00:50:36,720 --> 00:50:38,300
work, then you can discard it quickly

1044
00:50:38,300 --> 00:50:40,800
try out another idea, and so forth

1045
00:50:40,800 --> 00:50:43,640
And for me, there was kind of lots of times in my PhD

1046
00:50:43,640 --> 00:50:48,740
where I tried out lots of ideas that didn't make it into papers

1047
00:50:48,740 --> 00:50:50,400
They failed

1048
00:50:50,400 --> 00:50:56,120
And I think that this allowed me to come upon some ideas that

1049
00:50:56,120 --> 00:50:56,660
worked well

1050
00:50:56,660 --> 00:50:57,660
I didn't always do this

1051
00:50:57,660 --> 00:51:00,960
Sometimes, I ended up banging my head against the wall

1052
00:51:00,960 --> 00:51:01,740
plenty of times

1053
00:51:01,740 --> 00:51:03,160
But this is something that I found

1054
00:51:03,160 --> 00:51:05,720
to be a good strategy for trying to iterate and get

1055
00:51:05,720 --> 00:51:07,800
through the bad ideas quickly so that you

1056
00:51:07,800 --> 00:51:12,960
can get to some good ideas

1057
00:51:12,960 --> 00:51:15,080
And then I think the last thing on this slide

1058
00:51:15,080 --> 00:51:18,300
is if you can try out lots of ideas, especially

1059
00:51:18,300 --> 00:51:19,800
at the beginning of the project, you

1060
00:51:19,800 --> 00:51:21,640
can try out lots of ideas at the beginning

1061
00:51:21,640 --> 00:51:24,620
and then not commit to any one of them

1062
00:51:24,620 --> 00:51:27,540
until you see some signs of life on that core unknown

1063
00:51:27,540 --> 00:51:30,120
And then if you find signs of life on one of those projects

1064
00:51:30,120 --> 00:51:32,380
then you can start to commit to that project

1065
00:51:32,380 --> 00:51:36,820
after you see some sort of initial result on it

1066
00:51:36,820 --> 00:51:39,220
So this is another brute force approach

1067
00:51:39,220 --> 00:51:41,100
to trying lots of different things

1068
00:51:41,100 --> 00:51:44,260
so that you can get through the bad ideas

1069
00:51:44,260 --> 00:51:47,320
So the question is, in problem-driven research

1070
00:51:47,320 --> 00:51:48,940
it's a bit more comfortable because you

1071
00:51:48,940 --> 00:51:50,120
can try out multiple ideas

1072
00:51:50,120 --> 00:51:54,980
Are there scenarios where there isn't like a ton of ideas

1073
00:51:54,980 --> 00:51:57,580
And yeah, there's definitely scenarios

1074
00:51:57,580 --> 00:52:01,140
like that where you may not have tons

1075
00:52:01,140 --> 00:52:03,220
of ideas for solving a problem

1076
00:52:03,220 --> 00:52:06,990
And I should mention that in this approach, where you try out

1077
00:52:06,990 --> 00:52:08,660
lots of different things, you could also

1078
00:52:08,660 --> 00:52:11,380
be trying out multiple different problems as well

1079
00:52:11,380 --> 00:52:15,340
And so yeah, you could be trying out

1080
00:52:15,340 --> 00:52:17,080
like a couple ideas for one problem

1081
00:52:17,080 --> 00:52:19,380
try out a couple ideas for a different problem

1082
00:52:19,380 --> 00:52:25,130
and then pick your problem based on what seems to be tractable

1083
00:52:25,130 --> 00:52:27,350
Because even at the outset, you don't know how hard

1084
00:52:27,350 --> 00:52:28,610
a problem is going to be

1085
00:52:28,610 --> 00:52:32,050
And it might be that it's too hard for the scope

1086
00:52:32,050 --> 00:52:32,930
of a current project

1087
00:52:32,930 --> 00:52:37,310
Or we don't have good tools or good ideas for how to solve it

1088
00:52:37,310 --> 00:52:39,510
Can I give an example of frontloading a core unknown

1089
00:52:39,510 --> 00:52:41,030
in a project

1090
00:52:41,030 --> 00:52:42,330
A couple ideas

1091
00:52:42,330 --> 00:52:44,890
So maybe I'll try to give a couple examples

1092
00:52:44,890 --> 00:52:50,170
So one example is I worked on meta learning in the past

1093
00:52:50,170 --> 00:52:53,390
And the goal was to see if you can quickly

1094
00:52:53,390 --> 00:52:58,170
adapt with a very small amount of data to a problem

1095
00:52:58,170 --> 00:53:00,270
And the goal was ultimately to be

1096
00:53:00,270 --> 00:53:02,870
able to get a robot, for example, to quickly adapt

1097
00:53:02,870 --> 00:53:06,520
to a new task

1098
00:53:06,520 --> 00:53:09,830
But actually, designing a meta learning system on a real robot

1099
00:53:09,830 --> 00:53:12,910
is a ton of work and would take a lot of work

1100
00:53:12,910 --> 00:53:15,030
to even just do the coding for it

1101
00:53:15,030 --> 00:53:20,650
And so what I did is I first set up an experiment where

1102
00:53:20,650 --> 00:53:24,550
the tasks-- instead of learning robot tasks

1103
00:53:24,550 --> 00:53:30,290
the tasks were regressing to simple 1D sinusoid curves

1104
00:53:30,290 --> 00:53:31,890
So really, really simple problem

1105
00:53:31,890 --> 00:53:35,030
And then just tested, can you learn with a few data points

1106
00:53:35,030 --> 00:53:38,850
like five data points, to regress to the curve

1107
00:53:38,850 --> 00:53:43,130
And so it was this experiment that I coded up in a day

1108
00:53:43,130 --> 00:53:45,490
and get some initial results from

1109
00:53:45,490 --> 00:53:47,910
And it ended up working out pretty well

1110
00:53:47,910 --> 00:53:53,490
And so then that gave me the signs of life to try it

1111
00:53:53,490 --> 00:53:58,460
Another example of this, maybe a more recent example

1112
00:53:58,460 --> 00:54:00,170
I've been thinking a little bit about how

1113
00:54:00,170 --> 00:54:02,340
to get robots to follow different kinds of language

1114
00:54:02,340 --> 00:54:04,210
instructions, including language instructions

1115
00:54:04,210 --> 00:54:05,650
for different strategies

1116
00:54:05,650 --> 00:54:08,230
And there's a couple of risks for this

1117
00:54:08,230 --> 00:54:10,900
One is getting good labels for the strategies

1118
00:54:10,900 --> 00:54:12,730
And the second is actually getting the robot

1119
00:54:12,730 --> 00:54:18,480
to pay attention to those different strategies

1120
00:54:18,480 --> 00:54:19,940
And that second one actually seemed

1121
00:54:19,940 --> 00:54:21,730
like the bigger challenge

1122
00:54:21,730 --> 00:54:24,060
And I took a data set where it was like a really, really

1123
00:54:24,060 --> 00:54:26,090
simple problem, where in some of the data

1124
00:54:26,090 --> 00:54:27,460
the robot used the right gripper

1125
00:54:27,460 --> 00:54:29,640
And in some of the data, the robot used the left gripper

1126
00:54:29,640 --> 00:54:31,260
And then I trained a policy just to see

1127
00:54:31,260 --> 00:54:34,920
if it could do that very simple thing, very easy to evaluate

1128
00:54:34,920 --> 00:54:37,340
and I could automatically determine

1129
00:54:37,340 --> 00:54:39,300
if it was using the right or left from the data

1130
00:54:39,300 --> 00:54:40,600
rather than doing labeling

1131
00:54:40,600 --> 00:54:43,740
And so that kind of isolated a core risk of the problem

1132
00:54:43,740 --> 00:54:47,860
before moving on to the broader problem of getting a robot

1133
00:54:47,860 --> 00:54:51,120
to follow strategies

1134
00:54:51,120 --> 00:54:53,240
Now, you have a problem

1135
00:54:53,240 --> 00:54:54,240
You have an idea

1136
00:54:54,240 --> 00:54:56,000
You want to get things to work

1137
00:54:56,000 --> 00:54:58,420
One thing that's really hard in terms of getting something

1138
00:54:58,420 --> 00:55:00,250
to work is if you're starting with something

1139
00:55:00,250 --> 00:55:01,740
that doesn't work

1140
00:55:01,740 --> 00:55:04,620
So if you coded up a system and it's not working

1141
00:55:04,620 --> 00:55:08,900
there's a million reasons that could be why it's not working

1142
00:55:08,900 --> 00:55:11,860
And it's really hard to figure out which of those things

1143
00:55:11,860 --> 00:55:14,150
might be causing it to not work

1144
00:55:14,150 --> 00:55:15,320
You can make visualizations

1145
00:55:15,320 --> 00:55:18,480
There's various tools for trying to figure that out

1146
00:55:18,480 --> 00:55:20,860
But it's much easier to start with something that works

1147
00:55:20,860 --> 00:55:22,680
and then make it harder than to start

1148
00:55:22,680 --> 00:55:25,520
with something that doesn't work and try to get it to work

1149
00:55:25,520 --> 00:55:28,320
And so one thing that's very useful in research

1150
00:55:28,320 --> 00:55:31,080
is to-- if you're in a scenario where things aren't working

1151
00:55:31,080 --> 00:55:32,480
simplify the problem dramatically

1152
00:55:32,480 --> 00:55:33,980
until something does work

1153
00:55:33,980 --> 00:55:36,080
And then move it back to the harder setting

1154
00:55:36,080 --> 00:55:40,320
by, for example, adding more data, more tasks, whatever

1155
00:55:40,320 --> 00:55:43,560
And yeah, really useful debugging strategy

1156
00:55:43,560 --> 00:55:45,920
And I've seen, yeah, a lot of projects

1157
00:55:45,920 --> 00:55:47,320
where things aren't working

1158
00:55:47,320 --> 00:55:50,470
And you just kind of need to go back to the drawing board

1159
00:55:50,470 --> 00:55:53,580
Maybe as a very specific example for this, at one point

1160
00:55:53,580 --> 00:55:58,240
I was trying to train robots to be

1161
00:55:58,240 --> 00:56:01,160
able to see a video of a human doing a task

1162
00:56:01,160 --> 00:56:05,280
and then do the task themselves with imitation learning

1163
00:56:05,280 --> 00:56:08,260
Collected a really diverse data set of robot demonstrations

1164
00:56:08,260 --> 00:56:10,640
and then asked a human to do what the robot did

1165
00:56:10,640 --> 00:56:11,940
of all sorts of tasks

1166
00:56:11,940 --> 00:56:15,210
And we just told people collecting the data

1167
00:56:15,210 --> 00:56:16,150
do whatever you want

1168
00:56:16,150 --> 00:56:18,090
Do lots of things

1169
00:56:18,090 --> 00:56:21,490
Nothing worked when training on this data

1170
00:56:21,490 --> 00:56:26,810
Spent like months trying to get the robot to do it

1171
00:56:26,810 --> 00:56:30,730
And then what we did is we simplified to just a single task

1172
00:56:30,730 --> 00:56:31,230
setting

1173
00:56:31,230 --> 00:56:32,990
Like, can we get the robot to do one task

1174
00:56:32,990 --> 00:56:35,030
and make sure our imitation learning stack works

1175
00:56:35,030 --> 00:56:36,130
It actually didn't work

1176
00:56:36,130 --> 00:56:39,050
And so we had to debug that, get it to work on one task

1177
00:56:39,050 --> 00:56:40,670
then get it to work on three tasks

1178
00:56:40,670 --> 00:56:44,050
then get it to work on 10 tasks

1179
00:56:44,050 --> 00:56:45,930
So yeah, starting with something that

1180
00:56:45,930 --> 00:56:48,210
works-- really, really helpful

1181
00:56:48,210 --> 00:56:50,970
Simplifying is really helpful for that

1182
00:56:50,970 --> 00:56:54,570
Another thing, it's really easy to get in your own head

1183
00:56:54,570 --> 00:56:59,190
and just have trouble debugging something just by yourself

1184
00:56:59,190 --> 00:57:01,270
And so talking to friends, colleagues

1185
00:57:01,270 --> 00:57:03,770
advisors can also be helpful and help

1186
00:57:03,770 --> 00:57:06,850
you figure out whether it makes sense to jump up a level

1187
00:57:06,850 --> 00:57:10,550
and reconsider the problem or keep on tuning hyperparameters

1188
00:57:10,550 --> 00:57:13,050
or try something that you didn't think of

1189
00:57:13,050 --> 00:57:16,160
And then also, revisiting assumptions

1190
00:57:16,160 --> 00:57:18,060
So you might go into a project thinking

1191
00:57:18,060 --> 00:57:22,280
that something is a lot easier than it turned out to be

1192
00:57:22,280 --> 00:57:24,700
And then you might learn through the course of the project

1193
00:57:24,700 --> 00:57:27,020
that that thing is actually really hard

1194
00:57:27,020 --> 00:57:29,260
And so thinking through, like, has

1195
00:57:29,260 --> 00:57:31,880
anything changed about my assumptions from the beginning

1196
00:57:31,880 --> 00:57:33,960
And then if something has changed

1197
00:57:33,960 --> 00:57:35,580
would that change the way that you're

1198
00:57:35,580 --> 00:57:37,370
going about the project or even the project

1199
00:57:37,370 --> 00:57:40,260
that you're working on as a result

1200
00:57:40,260 --> 00:57:45,760
And then maybe I'll also talk about a story for this

1201
00:57:45,760 --> 00:57:49,500
So there's scenarios where you're trying

1202
00:57:49,500 --> 00:57:50,540
to get something to work

1203
00:57:50,540 --> 00:57:52,120
And it doesn't really want to work

1204
00:57:52,120 --> 00:57:54,300
It's not really easy to get it to work

1205
00:57:54,300 --> 00:57:57,780
And if it doesn't really want to work, then probably

1206
00:57:57,780 --> 00:57:59,490
the idea won't be very impactful

1207
00:57:59,490 --> 00:58:01,620
Because if someone else is trying to get it to work

1208
00:58:01,620 --> 00:58:04,340
and it also doesn't really work for them

1209
00:58:04,340 --> 00:58:08,940
then maybe the idea just wasn't really meant to be

1210
00:58:08,940 --> 00:58:11,960
And at one point, we had this project where--

1211
00:58:11,960 --> 00:58:13,890
this is actually a fairly recent project

1212
00:58:13,890 --> 00:58:15,760
where we were trying to get a robot to follow

1213
00:58:15,760 --> 00:58:19,800
open-ended language commands

1214
00:58:19,800 --> 00:58:22,920
Can you clean up the trash but not the dishes

1215
00:58:22,920 --> 00:58:25,440
We also wanted to see if the robot could handle

1216
00:58:25,440 --> 00:58:27,400
various interventions, like--

1217
00:58:27,400 --> 00:58:29,720
and follow lots of different prompts, for example

1218
00:58:29,720 --> 00:58:32,320
Then we kind of got some initial signs of life on that

1219
00:58:32,320 --> 00:58:34,200
And we're like, oh, well, maybe we could also

1220
00:58:34,200 --> 00:58:37,640
get the robot to ask questions if the prompt is ambiguous

1221
00:58:37,640 --> 00:58:40,040
And we had all these kind of cool ideas

1222
00:58:40,040 --> 00:58:43,520
for how we could incorporate the open-ended language following

1223
00:58:43,520 --> 00:58:46,200
in a robot system

1224
00:58:46,200 --> 00:58:50,360
And despite having positive initial signs

1225
00:58:50,360 --> 00:58:53,140
of life, after we introduced all those ideas

1226
00:58:53,140 --> 00:58:54,840
things weren't really working

1227
00:58:54,840 --> 00:58:58,440
And we kind of tried all sorts of-- we

1228
00:58:58,440 --> 00:59:00,520
spent a couple of months trying to get it to work

1229
00:59:00,520 --> 00:59:04,760
And at some point, we kind of went back

1230
00:59:04,760 --> 00:59:10,370
and thought about, well, OK, it was working before

1231
00:59:10,370 --> 00:59:13,030
What parts of this thing do work

1232
00:59:13,030 --> 00:59:14,450
And what parts of these things are

1233
00:59:14,450 --> 00:59:16,850
harder to work with our current method

1234
00:59:16,850 --> 00:59:21,010
And then we rescoped the project to remove the hard parts

1235
00:59:21,010 --> 00:59:22,750
that we didn't know how to solve yet

1236
00:59:22,750 --> 00:59:26,370
and to only consider the kind of interactions

1237
00:59:26,370 --> 00:59:28,330
that our method did work with

1238
00:59:28,330 --> 00:59:33,930
And so it was an example where we scoped the project

1239
00:59:33,930 --> 00:59:37,110
to the source of things that the method wanted to work on

1240
00:59:37,110 --> 00:59:39,410
it worked easily on, and left the things

1241
00:59:39,410 --> 00:59:41,150
that it didn't handle for future work

1242
00:59:41,150 --> 00:59:43,930
We described it as limitations in the paper

1243
00:59:43,930 --> 00:59:46,370
And so scoping down the project, especially

1244
00:59:46,370 --> 00:59:50,290
if you scoped it up a little bit too eagerly

1245
00:59:50,290 --> 00:59:55,690
can also be a useful strategy in research

1246
00:59:55,690 --> 00:59:58,450
Last thing, if you're in a scenario where things really

1247
00:59:58,450 --> 01:00:00,690
aren't working, you might decide whether or not

1248
01:00:00,690 --> 01:00:02,170
you should pivot or whether or not

1249
01:00:02,170 --> 01:00:05,450
you should start working on a different project

1250
01:00:05,450 --> 01:00:07,170
Oftentimes, you'll consider this later

1251
01:00:07,170 --> 01:00:10,350
than you should be considering this because you really

1252
01:00:10,350 --> 01:00:12,310
want to get something to work

1253
01:00:12,310 --> 01:00:13,950
There's also this sunk cost fallacy

1254
01:00:13,950 --> 01:00:17,930
where if you spent a lot of time on something, then

1255
01:00:17,930 --> 01:00:19,730
it's very natural for humans to think

1256
01:00:19,730 --> 01:00:21,690
that you'll spend less time on it in the future

1257
01:00:21,690 --> 01:00:23,870
because you spend a lot of time on it in the past

1258
01:00:23,870 --> 01:00:26,030
This is a fallacy, not always true

1259
01:00:26,030 --> 01:00:29,550
And you might actually not have made as much progress

1260
01:00:29,550 --> 01:00:33,350
on the problem than the time that you spent on

1261
01:00:33,350 --> 01:00:37,110
it would suggest

1262
01:00:37,110 --> 01:00:40,310
Now, deciding when to pivot is really hard because you're often

1263
01:00:40,310 --> 01:00:41,750
thinking about, should I continue

1264
01:00:41,750 --> 01:00:43,130
working on my current project

1265
01:00:43,130 --> 01:00:46,470
Or should I switch to starting a new project

1266
01:00:46,470 --> 01:00:49,990
And this option of switching to a new project

1267
01:00:49,990 --> 01:00:51,620
is really nebulous

1268
01:00:51,620 --> 01:00:53,370
This could mean a lot of different things

1269
01:00:53,370 --> 01:00:55,580
There's a lot of different things you might be doing

1270
01:00:55,580 --> 01:00:57,250
And so it's really hard to think about

1271
01:00:57,250 --> 01:00:59,290
should I be doing what I'm currently doing

1272
01:00:59,290 --> 01:01:00,630
Or should I do something else

1273
01:01:00,630 --> 01:01:04,670
Because something else could be anything

1274
01:01:04,670 --> 01:01:07,440
And so one kind of mental trick for thinking about

1275
01:01:07,440 --> 01:01:09,840
this sort of decision about whether to pivot

1276
01:01:09,840 --> 01:01:12,370
is to change this decision and actually

1277
01:01:12,370 --> 01:01:14,040
spend time thinking about other projects

1278
01:01:14,040 --> 01:01:16,960
that you would do and then try to formulate

1279
01:01:16,960 --> 01:01:18,760
the decision as continuing working

1280
01:01:18,760 --> 01:01:21,340
on the current project versus working on project B

1281
01:01:21,340 --> 01:01:24,240
or working on project C and so forth

1282
01:01:24,240 --> 01:01:27,600
And this decision is much more concrete, a lot more

1283
01:01:27,600 --> 01:01:31,120
straightforward to think about, less anxiety inducing, and so

1284
01:01:31,120 --> 01:01:33,000
forth

1285
01:01:33,000 --> 01:01:37,240
So the question is, when research fields are moving

1286
01:01:37,240 --> 01:01:42,560
really quickly, you might be worried that another group will

1287
01:01:42,560 --> 01:01:45,960
do the same thing and then try to spend less

1288
01:01:45,960 --> 01:01:48,840
time on the evaluation just to try to get the paper out faster

1289
01:01:48,840 --> 01:01:51,160
Is that a trend

1290
01:01:51,160 --> 01:01:53,440
What do I think about that

1291
01:01:53,440 --> 01:02:01,960
So there's definitely papers that have spent--

1292
01:02:01,960 --> 01:02:04,540
that should be more thorough with the evaluation

1293
01:02:04,540 --> 01:02:05,660
That's been true

1294
01:02:05,660 --> 01:02:06,480
That's true now

1295
01:02:06,480 --> 01:02:09,740
That's also been true in the past

1296
01:02:09,740 --> 01:02:15,420
I don't know if it's been getting worse per se

1297
01:02:15,420 --> 01:02:19,960
I think that if there is a feeling to do that

1298
01:02:19,960 --> 01:02:22,380
I think you really want to be confident about the results

1299
01:02:22,380 --> 01:02:23,900
that you're putting out

1300
01:02:23,900 --> 01:02:27,660
And I think that if you're worried about being scooped

1301
01:02:27,660 --> 01:02:35,630
or another group doing something

1302
01:02:35,630 --> 01:02:40,920
there is an advantage to having done some sort of idea first

1303
01:02:40,920 --> 01:02:43,900
But there's also a lot of advantage to executing really

1304
01:02:43,900 --> 01:02:45,780
well on a project

1305
01:02:45,780 --> 01:02:48,580
And sometimes, the projects that are executed on better

1306
01:02:48,580 --> 01:02:50,460
are more impactful than the projects

1307
01:02:50,460 --> 01:02:53,300
that were like first at a particular idea

1308
01:02:53,300 --> 01:02:59,700
And so I guess my perspective is to still put

1309
01:02:59,700 --> 01:03:03,410
a fair amount of focus on execution and evaluation

1310
01:03:03,410 --> 01:03:05,250
Now, there's many things you could evaluate

1311
01:03:05,250 --> 01:03:07,510
You could run your experiments on 20 data sets

1312
01:03:07,510 --> 01:03:10,870
And so there's a limit to what you want to do

1313
01:03:10,870 --> 01:03:14,070
So I think there's a balance

1314
01:03:14,070 --> 01:03:19,070
And certainly, there are some parts of the research field

1315
01:03:19,070 --> 01:03:20,130
that move fast

1316
01:03:20,130 --> 01:03:22,590
One thing that I've liked about real robots

1317
01:03:22,590 --> 01:03:26,350
is that a small enough people work with like real hardware

1318
01:03:26,350 --> 01:03:29,240
that you have to worry-- you can worry less about that

1319
01:03:29,240 --> 01:03:31,110
in many ways, especially if you execute on it

1320
01:03:31,110 --> 01:03:35,890
and actually show the results on real robots

1321
01:03:35,890 --> 01:03:36,860
But there's a balance

1322
01:03:36,860 --> 01:03:39,230
And it's also good to work on things like language models

1323
01:03:39,230 --> 01:03:41,340
and things that are hot too

1324
01:03:41,340 --> 01:03:46,790
Yeah, there's a balance

1325
01:03:46,790 --> 01:03:48,830
So how to share your research

1326
01:03:48,830 --> 01:03:52,730
I talked about why is this really important

1327
01:03:52,730 --> 01:03:56,350
So if you think about what is the output or the outcome

1328
01:03:56,350 --> 01:03:59,390
of research, the output of research

1329
01:03:59,390 --> 01:04:03,370
is essentially ideas, knowledge, things that you learned

1330
01:04:03,370 --> 01:04:04,930
from doing the research

1331
01:04:04,930 --> 01:04:06,550
It's not a product

1332
01:04:06,550 --> 01:04:07,710
It's not a service

1333
01:04:07,710 --> 01:04:08,790
It's really knowledge

1334
01:04:08,790 --> 01:04:10,810
It's things that you learn

1335
01:04:10,810 --> 01:04:17,180
And if it were a product, then the high impact

1336
01:04:17,180 --> 01:04:18,850
would mean that lots of people are using

1337
01:04:18,850 --> 01:04:20,690
the product for good things

1338
01:04:20,690 --> 01:04:22,410
But since it's more like knowledge

1339
01:04:22,410 --> 01:04:26,170
if no one knows about what you learned, then there was really

1340
01:04:26,170 --> 01:04:31,030
no impact or no output as a result. And even for companies

1341
01:04:31,030 --> 01:04:32,570
that have products and services, they

1342
01:04:32,570 --> 01:04:34,110
have massive marketing efforts to try

1343
01:04:34,110 --> 01:04:35,930
to get those products out

1344
01:04:35,930 --> 01:04:38,750
And so for your work to have impact, you need to share it

1345
01:04:38,750 --> 01:04:40,450
And you need people to know about it

1346
01:04:40,450 --> 01:04:43,210
Otherwise, it won't have had impact

1347
01:04:43,210 --> 01:04:48,110
Now, there can be reasons to not be excited about sharing work

1348
01:04:48,110 --> 01:04:50,570
You can say maybe it feels like self-promotion

1349
01:04:50,570 --> 01:04:53,010
Maybe the idea didn't work that well so I'm not

1350
01:04:53,010 --> 01:04:54,510
that excited about sharing it

1351
01:04:54,510 --> 01:04:56,610
Maybe it seems all obvious

1352
01:04:56,610 --> 01:04:58,820
All of it seems obvious to you now

1353
01:04:58,820 --> 01:05:00,580
And so why do you need to share it

1354
01:05:00,580 --> 01:05:02,960
But even if it feels like self-promotion

1355
01:05:02,960 --> 01:05:03,920
you're teaching people

1356
01:05:03,920 --> 01:05:05,620
You're sharing cool new findings

1357
01:05:05,620 --> 01:05:08,080
And you can think about it as more actually doing a service

1358
01:05:08,080 --> 01:05:11,620
to them to teach them something new and useful and interesting

1359
01:05:11,620 --> 01:05:13,240
Even if it didn't work that well

1360
01:05:13,240 --> 01:05:15,740
it's really useful to share because other people might

1361
01:05:15,740 --> 01:05:17,540
think of that idea and might want

1362
01:05:17,540 --> 01:05:19,080
to know how well did it work

1363
01:05:19,080 --> 01:05:21,200
And did it work in certain circumstances

1364
01:05:21,200 --> 01:05:24,020
more better than other circumstances

1365
01:05:24,020 --> 01:05:25,640
And then, yeah, after enough research

1366
01:05:25,640 --> 01:05:27,840
you're going to know a lot more than other people

1367
01:05:27,840 --> 01:05:30,380
There will be things that seem really obvious to you that are

1368
01:05:30,380 --> 01:05:32,020
not obvious to other people

1369
01:05:32,020 --> 01:05:34,580
And it's good to still share those things

1370
01:05:34,580 --> 01:05:38,460
and think about what actually other people don't know

1371
01:05:38,460 --> 01:05:41,380
so that you can share the things that you

1372
01:05:41,380 --> 01:05:43,500
have learned, especially the most important things

1373
01:05:43,500 --> 01:05:45,860
with them

1374
01:05:45,860 --> 01:05:50,580
Now, I could probably give an entire lecture

1375
01:05:50,580 --> 01:05:54,220
on presenting research and writing and presentations and so

1376
01:05:54,220 --> 01:05:55,220
forth

1377
01:05:55,220 --> 01:05:56,960
But yeah, having really clear writing

1378
01:05:56,960 --> 01:05:59,760
really clear visuals, really clear presentations

1379
01:05:59,760 --> 01:06:02,120
Communicating clearly is really, really important

1380
01:06:02,120 --> 01:06:03,980
If they don't understand what you're saying

1381
01:06:03,980 --> 01:06:06,410
then they won't understand the knowledge

1382
01:06:06,410 --> 01:06:08,240
It's important to think about their audience

1383
01:06:08,240 --> 01:06:11,040
and how they will interpret what you say, what they know

1384
01:06:11,040 --> 01:06:12,760
When in doubt, assume that your audience

1385
01:06:12,760 --> 01:06:15,180
knows less rather than more

1386
01:06:15,180 --> 01:06:17,140
This can actually be a little bit tricky to do

1387
01:06:17,140 --> 01:06:19,380
It's good to avoid jargon if possible

1388
01:06:19,380 --> 01:06:20,720
They might know the jargon

1389
01:06:20,720 --> 01:06:22,680
But if there's ways to avoid it, then it

1390
01:06:22,680 --> 01:06:24,600
makes it more accessible

1391
01:06:24,600 --> 01:06:26,640
And even if they do know a lot of what

1392
01:06:26,640 --> 01:06:28,280
you're going to say, a lot of people

1393
01:06:28,280 --> 01:06:30,480
still appreciate refreshers, especially

1394
01:06:30,480 --> 01:06:32,220
if those refreshers are clear

1395
01:06:32,220 --> 01:06:34,600
And it might actually cause them to think about something

1396
01:06:34,600 --> 01:06:37,220
that they know in a new way

1397
01:06:37,220 --> 01:06:38,840
Other ways to get better at this-- just

1398
01:06:38,840 --> 01:06:41,240
practicing-- practicing writing, practicing, giving

1399
01:06:41,240 --> 01:06:42,560
presentations

1400
01:06:42,560 --> 01:06:43,620
You can practice alone

1401
01:06:43,620 --> 01:06:47,040
You can practice with other people if it's a presentation

1402
01:06:47,040 --> 01:06:48,940
And then just try to get feedback

1403
01:06:48,940 --> 01:06:50,560
Try to get honest feedback from people

1404
01:06:50,560 --> 01:06:54,280
and improve with the feedback

1405
01:06:54,280 --> 01:06:57,890
Now, when trying to write a paper

1406
01:06:57,890 --> 01:07:00,640
or when trying to create a slide deck or something

1407
01:07:00,640 --> 01:07:02,350
it's easy to get a little bit overwhelmed

1408
01:07:02,350 --> 01:07:04,930
by how much you have to do and where

1409
01:07:04,930 --> 01:07:06,850
even to get started on that

1410
01:07:06,850 --> 01:07:09,050
I find it really helpful to break down the task

1411
01:07:09,050 --> 01:07:12,210
And even on a pen and paper, just write down some ideas

1412
01:07:12,210 --> 01:07:14,170
for how I want to do it

1413
01:07:14,170 --> 01:07:16,050
Maybe write down an outline

1414
01:07:16,050 --> 01:07:18,330
My recommendation would be to think through your ideas

1415
01:07:18,330 --> 01:07:20,980
for what you want to communicate before asking your friend

1416
01:07:20,980 --> 01:07:25,090
before asking your friend ChatGPT

1417
01:07:25,090 --> 01:07:27,130
And then a couple miscellaneous things

1418
01:07:27,130 --> 01:07:28,590
First is mentorship

1419
01:07:28,590 --> 01:07:31,630
I think mentorship can play a huge role in research

1420
01:07:31,630 --> 01:07:35,290
And if you have it, don't be afraid to lean on it

1421
01:07:35,290 --> 01:07:38,350
In my first research paper in my PhD

1422
01:07:38,350 --> 01:07:42,670
I like hardly wrote any of the paper

1423
01:07:42,670 --> 01:07:44,280
And I talked to actually my co-author

1424
01:07:44,280 --> 01:07:46,610
And he told me that he actually even rewrote the section

1425
01:07:46,610 --> 01:07:49,050
that I wrote

1426
01:07:49,050 --> 01:07:51,730
And so it's OK to not know everything and not

1427
01:07:51,730 --> 01:07:54,610
know how to do every part of the research process

1428
01:07:54,610 --> 01:07:56,710
And lean on other people for the things

1429
01:07:56,710 --> 01:07:58,750
that you don't know how to do yet

1430
01:07:58,750 --> 01:08:02,110
Generally, I've seen students and myself

1431
01:08:02,110 --> 01:08:04,830
do most successful when they're learning more gradually

1432
01:08:04,830 --> 01:08:07,380
and when they're not trying to take on everything themselves

1433
01:08:07,380 --> 01:08:09,750
because doing novel research and writing a paper about it

1434
01:08:09,750 --> 01:08:12,390
and presenting it-- like, picking a problem, all of that

1435
01:08:12,390 --> 01:08:14,950
is a really, really huge undertaking

1436
01:08:14,950 --> 01:08:17,229
And it's useful to lean on other people

1437
01:08:17,229 --> 01:08:22,229
as you're trying to learn how to do that

1438
01:08:22,229 --> 01:08:23,490
And then confidence

1439
01:08:23,490 --> 01:08:27,830
So there's a lot of reasons to lack confidence in research

1440
01:08:27,830 --> 01:08:30,270
No one knows how to do research right now

1441
01:08:30,270 --> 01:08:32,910
No one knows what research is going to be most impactful

1442
01:08:32,910 --> 01:08:35,270
If they did, it wouldn't be research

1443
01:08:35,270 --> 01:08:36,370
Many ideas don't work

1444
01:08:36,370 --> 01:08:38,210
Many papers get rejected

1445
01:08:38,210 --> 01:08:39,670
Many researchers have been thinking

1446
01:08:39,670 --> 01:08:42,029
about a domain for many years

1447
01:08:42,029 --> 01:08:44,830
And they will be smarter than you in that domain

1448
01:08:44,830 --> 01:08:47,990
because they've been thinking about it for many years

1449
01:08:47,990 --> 01:08:51,560
And so it's easy to think that you know very little, that you

1450
01:08:51,560 --> 01:08:54,680
have been rejected many times, that a lot of your ideas

1451
01:08:54,680 --> 01:08:58,010
don't work, because all of those things are kind of true

1452
01:08:58,010 --> 01:08:59,760
But that doesn't mean that you're actually

1453
01:08:59,760 --> 01:09:02,120
doing a bad job at research

1454
01:09:02,120 --> 01:09:03,899
And confidence is really important

1455
01:09:03,899 --> 01:09:05,319
If you're not confident, if you're

1456
01:09:05,319 --> 01:09:08,220
second guessing, overthinking things

1457
01:09:08,220 --> 01:09:10,120
it can really slow you down

1458
01:09:10,120 --> 01:09:14,300
And even though these things are challenging

1459
01:09:14,300 --> 01:09:20,960
they're actually not reasons to be unconfident

1460
01:09:20,960 --> 01:09:23,720
And then lastly, some closing thoughts

1461
01:09:23,720 --> 01:09:26,319
Maybe since you're all here, you're eager to do research

1462
01:09:26,319 --> 01:09:27,899
And so maybe I don't need to say this

1463
01:09:27,899 --> 01:09:32,180
But in terms of the time that we're in right now

1464
01:09:32,180 --> 01:09:38,520
I think that you might feel like reinforcement learning works now

1465
01:09:38,520 --> 01:09:39,380
to some extent

1466
01:09:39,380 --> 01:09:41,340
And so maybe we don't need to do research

1467
01:09:41,340 --> 01:09:45,970
Maybe we can just use the thing that works

1468
01:09:45,970 --> 01:09:49,060
and apply it in all sorts of circumstances

1469
01:09:49,060 --> 01:09:52,220
And there's a lot of merit to applying research

1470
01:09:52,220 --> 01:09:54,260
in the real world

1471
01:09:54,260 --> 01:09:56,420
It's good for some people to do that

1472
01:09:56,420 --> 01:09:58,340
But at the same time, I think that now

1473
01:09:58,340 --> 01:10:01,380
is a better time than ever to do research because we are actually

1474
01:10:01,380 --> 01:10:03,460
seeing new research being translated

1475
01:10:03,460 --> 01:10:06,100
into very real-world impact

1476
01:10:06,100 --> 01:10:09,100
And a lot of the big breakthroughs

1477
01:10:09,100 --> 01:10:14,500
like AlphaFold, like ChatGPT, and so forth

1478
01:10:14,500 --> 01:10:16,580
compared to the breakthroughs of like 10 years ago

1479
01:10:16,580 --> 01:10:18,060
or 20 years ago, these are things

1480
01:10:18,060 --> 01:10:21,780
that are actually leading to really massive impact

1481
01:10:21,780 --> 01:10:23,020
in the world today

1482
01:10:23,020 --> 01:10:26,220
And if you work on research now, you

1483
01:10:26,220 --> 01:10:29,220
can help contribute to those kinds of new breakthroughs

1484
01:10:29,220 --> 01:10:32,260
that will have even more impact in the future compared

1485
01:10:32,260 --> 01:10:34,630
to if you were doing research 10 or 20 years ago

1486
01:10:34,630 --> 01:10:36,500
So I think that now is actually a better time

1487
01:10:36,500 --> 01:10:39,700
than ever to be doing research, including in deep reinforcement

1488
01:10:39,700 --> 01:10:41,300
learning

1489
01:10:41,300 --> 01:10:44,450
So yeah, those are my final thoughts

